-- 1. [Bond Length & Bond Energy · Skill 2 · ans A] id 2a060723-c691-4ea5-a7e6-1d34c914a506
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Acetylene undergoes complete hydrogenation to ethane: \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_2(g) + 2\,\text{H}_2(g) \rightarrow \text{C}_2\text{H}_6(g)\), with a measured \(\Delta H = -317\ \text{kJ}\,\text{mol}^{-1}\). Acetylene has the connectivity \(\text{H}-\text{C}\equiv\text{C}-\text{H}\) and ethane is \(\text{H}_3\text{C}-\text{CH}_3\). Using the average bond energies \(\text{C}\equiv\text{C} = 839\), \(\text{H}-\text{H} = 436\), and \(\text{C}-\text{C} = 348\) (all in \(\text{kJ}\,\text{mol}^{-1}\)), the value of the average \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bond energy that is most consistent with this \(\Delta H\) is closest to$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(420\\ \\text{kJ}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(280\\ \\text{kJ}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(311\\ \\text{kJ}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(507\\ \\text{kJ}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the bond-energy estimate of reaction enthalpy, \(\Delta H = \sum(\text{bonds broken}) - \sum(\text{bonds formed})\), must be set up with the correct bond inventory and then solved algebraically for the one unknown, the average \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) energy \(y\). In the reactants, \(\text{H}-\text{C}\equiv\text{C}-\text{H}\) contributes one \(\text{C}\equiv\text{C}\) and two \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds, and the two \(\text{H}_2\) molecules contribute two \(\text{H}-\text{H}\) bonds, so the bonds broken sum to \(839 + 2(436) + 2y = 1711 + 2y\). In the product \(\text{H}_3\text{C}-\text{CH}_3\), one \(\text{C}-\text{C}\) and six \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds form, so the bonds formed sum to \(348 + 6y\). Thus \(\Delta H = (1711 + 2y) - (348 + 6y) = 1363 - 4y\). The two \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds that break partially cancel against the six that form, leaving a net coefficient of \(-4\). Setting this equal to the measured value, \(-317 = 1363 - 4y\), gives \(4y = 1680\), so \(y = 420\ \text{kJ}\,\text{mol}^{-1}\), consistent with the typical average \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bond energy. (Choice B) of \(280\ \text{kJ}\,\text{mol}^{-1}\) arises from omitting the two \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds that break in acetylene, so the \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) term is treated as appearing only on the product side; this yields the equation \(1363 - 6y = -317\) and the net coefficient \(-6\) instead of \(-4\), overstating how strongly the unknown is weighted. (Choice C) of \(311\ \text{kJ}\,\text{mol}^{-1}\) results from breaking only one of the two \(\text{H}-\text{H}\) bonds, dropping one \(436\) term so the constant becomes \(927\) rather than \(1363\); solving \(927 - 4y = -317\) gives \(311\) and undercounts the energy absorbed when both \(\text{H}_2\) molecules dissociate. (Choice D) of \(507\ \text{kJ}\,\text{mol}^{-1}\) follows from omitting the \(\text{C}-\text{C}\) single bond that forms in ethane, leaving the constant as \(839 + 2(436) = 1711\) and the equation \(1711 - 4y = -317\); this neglects the energy released when the carbon-carbon single bond is created. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must build a correct bond inventory for two different molecules, recognize how the shared \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds partially cancel, and then carry an intermediate sum into an algebraic solution for the unknown bond energy.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '2a060723-c691-4ea5-a7e6-1d34c914a506';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2a060723-c691-4ea5-a7e6-1d34c914a506';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2a060723-c691-4ea5-a7e6-1d34c914a506', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Drops the partial cancellation of C-H bonds by ignoring the two C-H bonds broken in acetylene, using a net coefficient of 6 instead of 4 for the unknown$a$),
  ('2a060723-c691-4ea5-a7e6-1d34c914a506', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Omits one of the two H-H bonds broken (drops a stoichiometric coefficient on H2), shrinking the bonds-broken constant by 436$a$),
  ('2a060723-c691-4ea5-a7e6-1d34c914a506', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Forgets the C-C single bond that forms in the ethane product, omitting the 348 term from the bonds-formed sum$a$);

-- 2. [Bond Length & Bond Energy · Skill 2 · ans C] id a18d5c25-0c73-4bea-b540-a0853cb239d2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A reference table lists three carbon-carbon bonds: \(\text{C-C}\) at \(154\ \text{pm}\) and \(348\ \text{kJ/mol}\), \(\text{C=C}\) at \(134\ \text{pm}\) and \(614\ \text{kJ/mol}\), and \(\text{C} \equiv \text{C}\) at \(120\ \text{pm}\) and \(839\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Treating the single-bond value as the energy of one carbon-carbon \(\sigma\) bond and the additional energy in the double and triple bonds as arising solely from added \(\pi\) bonds, which conclusion about the individual \(\pi\)-bond contributions is best supported by these data?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"The three shared pairs contribute equally, each worth about \\(280\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\), so the \\(\\pi\\) bonds and the \\(\\sigma\\) bond are of essentially the same strength."},{"label":"B","text":"Each \\(\\pi\\) bond is individually stronger than the \\(\\sigma\\) bond, and the second \\(\\pi\\) bond (\\(266\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\)) is stronger than the first (\\(225\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\)), so successive \\(\\pi\\) contributions grow as the bond shortens."},{"label":"C","text":"Each \\(\\pi\\) bond is individually weaker than the \\(\\sigma\\) bond, and the second \\(\\pi\\) bond (\\(225\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\)) is weaker than the first (\\(266\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\)), so successive \\(\\pi\\) contributions diminish even as the bond keeps shortening."},{"label":"D","text":"Each added pair contributes the full \\(348\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\) of the \\(\\sigma\\) bond, so a triple bond should store about \\(1044\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\) and the tabulated \\(839\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\) must understate its true strength."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the total bond energy must first be decomposed into its \(\sigma\) and \(\pi\) components before any per-bond comparison can be made. Taking the single-bond value as one \(\sigma\) bond, the \(\sigma\) contribution is \(348\ \text{kJ/mol}\). The double bond (\(614\ \text{kJ/mol}\)) is one \(\sigma\) plus one \(\pi\), so the first \(\pi\) bond is \(614 - 348 = 266\ \text{kJ/mol}\). The triple bond (\(839\ \text{kJ/mol}\)) is one \(\sigma\) plus two \(\pi\), so the second \(\pi\) bond is \(839 - 614 = 225\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Both \(\pi\) values (\(266\) and \(225\)) fall below the \(348\ \text{kJ/mol}\) of the \(\sigma\) bond, and the second \(\pi\) (\(225\)) is smaller than the first (\(266\)), so each successive \(\pi\) bond adds less energy. This is the expected result because \(\pi\) overlap of side-by-side \(p\) orbitals is less effective than the head-on overlap of a \(\sigma\) bond, and it holds even though the internuclear distance keeps shrinking (\(154 \rightarrow 134 \rightarrow 120\ \text{pm}\)); the bond as a whole still gets shorter and stronger overall, but the marginal strengthening per added \(\pi\) bond declines. (Choice A) Dividing the triple-bond energy by three (\(839/3 \approx 280\ \text{kJ/mol}\)) assumes all three shared pairs are equivalent, but the single-bond datum fixes the \(\sigma\) bond at \(348\ \text{kJ/mol}\), well above any per-pair average, so the pairs are not equal and the \(\sigma\) bond is stronger than either \(\pi\) bond. (Choice B) This reverses both findings: it claims the \(\pi\) bonds exceed the \(\sigma\) bond, yet \(266\) and \(225\) are both below \(348\ \text{kJ/mol}\), and it swaps the two \(\pi\) values, asserting the second \(\pi\) (correctly \(225\)) is the larger \(266\); the differences \(614-348\) and \(839-614\) show the first \(\pi\) is the stronger one. (Choice D) Treating every shared pair as an independent \(348\ \text{kJ/mol}\) \(\sigma\) bond predicts \(3 \times 348 = 1044\ \text{kJ/mol}\) for the triple bond, but the tabulated \(839\ \text{kJ/mol}\) is the measured value; the shortfall is real and reflects that the two \(\pi\) bonds are each weaker than the \(\sigma\) bond, not that the table understates the strength. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must partition a measured total into separate \(\sigma\) and \(\pi\) contributions through successive differences and then compare those intermediate values to judge how individual \(\pi\)-bond strengths trend.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'a18d5c25-0c73-4bea-b540-a0853cb239d2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a18d5c25-0c73-4bea-b540-a0853cb239d2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a18d5c25-0c73-4bea-b540-a0853cb239d2', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Divides the triple-bond energy evenly by three pairs (839/3 approx 280) instead of subtracting the sigma reference, treating all shared pairs as equivalent.$a$),
  ('a18d5c25-0c73-4bea-b540-a0853cb239d2', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the sigma/pi strength ordering and swaps the two computed pi values, concluding pi bonds exceed the sigma bond and successive pi bonds grow stronger.$a$),
  ('a18d5c25-0c73-4bea-b540-a0853cb239d2', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Assumes bond energies are additive with each pair worth a full sigma bond (3 x 348 = 1044), then dismisses the lower tabulated value as an error rather than evidence of weaker pi bonds.$a$);

-- 3. [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · Skill 4 · ans A] id 1ec241ed-6229-4c6b-9b18-f2cf95c3304c
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist models four isolated single bonds as point charges, taking the bond dipole moment as \(\mu = \delta \times d\), where \(\delta\) is the partial charge (the percent ionic character times one electronic charge, \(e = 1.602 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{C}\)) and \(d\) is the bond length. The data are \(\text{C}-\text{Cl}\) (\(22\%\) ionic, \(178\ \text{pm}\)), \(\text{C}-\text{Br}\) (\(17\%\) ionic, \(194\ \text{pm}\)), \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\) (\(18\%\) ionic, \(128\ \text{pm}\)), and \(\text{H}-\text{Br}\) (\(12\%\) ionic, \(141\ \text{pm}\)). Ranked from largest to smallest bond dipole moment, these four bonds most likely fall in which order?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{C}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{C}-\\text{Br} > \\text{H}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{H}-\\text{Br}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{C}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{H}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{C}-\\text{Br} > \\text{H}-\\text{Br}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{C}-\\text{Br} > \\text{C}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{H}-\\text{Br} > \\text{H}-\\text{Cl}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{H}-\\text{Br} > \\text{H}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{C}-\\text{Br} > \\text{C}-\\text{Cl}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the bond dipole moment is the product of the separated partial charge and the distance over which it is separated, \(\mu = \delta \times d\), so a correct ranking cannot be read from electronegativity (percent ionic character) alone; the bond length must be multiplied in. Converting each percent ionic character to a partial charge \(\delta = (\text{percent ionic}/100)\times e\) and multiplying by the bond length gives, in units of \(10^{-30}\ \text{C}\,\text{m}\): \(\text{C}-\text{Cl} = 0.22 \times (1.602\times10^{-19})\times(178\times10^{-12}) \approx 6.27\) (about \(1.88\ \text{D}\)); \(\text{C}-\text{Br} = 0.17 \times (1.602\times10^{-19})\times(194\times10^{-12}) \approx 5.28\) (about \(1.58\ \text{D}\)); \(\text{H}-\text{Cl} = 0.18 \times (1.602\times10^{-19})\times(128\times10^{-12}) \approx 3.69\) (about \(1.11\ \text{D}\)); and \(\text{H}-\text{Br} = 0.12 \times (1.602\times10^{-19})\times(141\times10^{-12}) \approx 2.71\) (about \(0.81\ \text{D}\)). Ranking these products gives \(\text{C}-\text{Cl} > \text{C}-\text{Br} > \text{H}-\text{Cl} > \text{H}-\text{Br}\). The decisive comparison is \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\) versus \(\text{C}-\text{Br}\): \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\) is the more ionic bond (\(18\%\) versus \(17\%\)), yet \(\text{C}-\text{Br}\) carries the larger dipole because its much longer bond length (\(194\ \text{pm}\) versus \(128\ \text{pm}\)) more than compensates for its slightly smaller partial charge. (Choice B) This order ranks the four bonds by percent ionic character alone (\(22 > 18 > 17 > 12\)), treating the partial charge as the only factor and discarding the bond-length term of \(\mu = \delta \times d\); doing so wrongly places \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\) above \(\text{C}-\text{Br}\), even though the longer \(\text{C}-\text{Br}\) bond produces the larger charge separation distance and thus the larger dipole. (Choice C) This order ranks the bonds by bond length alone (\(194 > 178 > 141 > 128\ \text{pm}\)), treating distance as the only factor and discarding the partial-charge term; this wrongly elevates \(\text{C}-\text{Br}\) above \(\text{C}-\text{Cl}\) and \(\text{H}-\text{Br}\) above \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\), because a longer bond increases the dipole only when the partial charge is held comparable. (Choice D) This order is the exact reversal of the correct ranking, the sequence obtained by ordering the computed dipole products from smallest to largest rather than largest to smallest; \(\text{H}-\text{Br}\), with both the smallest partial charge and a short bond, has the smallest dipole, not the largest. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must convert each percent ionic character into a partial charge, multiply it by the corresponding bond length, and compare the four resulting products to resolve a ranking that the polarity data alone would get wrong.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '1ec241ed-6229-4c6b-9b18-f2cf95c3304c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1ec241ed-6229-4c6b-9b18-f2cf95c3304c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1ec241ed-6229-4c6b-9b18-f2cf95c3304c', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Ranks by percent ionic character (partial charge) alone, dropping the bond-length factor of mu = delta x d, so H-Cl is wrongly placed above the longer C-Br$a$),
  ('1ec241ed-6229-4c6b-9b18-f2cf95c3304c', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Ranks by bond length alone, treating the longest bond as the largest dipole and ignoring the partial-charge factor$a$),
  ('1ec241ed-6229-4c6b-9b18-f2cf95c3304c', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Computes the correct dipole products but reports them smallest-to-largest, reversing the requested largest-to-smallest direction$a$);

-- 4. [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · Skill 4 · ans B] id db7119f9-99e8-4221-bc64-f7331b51ec5b
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A reference card lists, for four isolated single bonds, the electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) and the internuclear distance \(d\): \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) at \(\Delta\text{EN}=1.94\) and \(d=131\,\text{pm}\); \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\) at \(\Delta\text{EN}=1.55\) and \(d=214\,\text{pm}\); \(\text{Si}-\text{O}\) at \(\Delta\text{EN}=1.54\) and \(d=163\,\text{pm}\); and \(\text{Al}-\text{Br}\) at \(\Delta\text{EN}=1.35\) and \(d=230\,\text{pm}\). The card states that the magnitude of the partial charge generated at each end is \(\delta=0.16\,\Delta\text{EN}\) (in units of the elementary charge) and that the bond dipole moment is \(\mu=\delta\times d\). Using only these data, the bond with the largest dipole moment is most likely$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"the \\(\\text{B}-\\text{F}\\) bond, because its \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}=1.94\\) is the largest, giving the greatest partial charge of the four."},{"label":"B","text":"the \\(\\text{Al}-\\text{Cl}\\) bond, because the product \\(\\delta\\times d\\approx 53\\) (in units of \\(e\\cdot\\text{pm}\\)) is the largest of the four."},{"label":"C","text":"the \\(\\text{Si}-\\text{O}\\) bond, because its \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}=1.54\\) is essentially tied with the most polar oxygen-containing alternative while its bond is not the longest."},{"label":"D","text":"the \\(\\text{Al}-\\text{Br}\\) bond, because its \\(d=230\\,\\text{pm}\\) is the largest, giving the greatest charge separation distance of the four."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the bond dipole moment is the product of the partial charge and the bond length, \(\mu=\delta\times d\), so neither factor alone fixes the ranking; both must be carried through. First convert each \(\Delta\text{EN}\) to a partial charge using \(\delta=0.16\,\Delta\text{EN}\): \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) gives \(\delta=0.310\), \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\) gives \(\delta=0.248\), \(\text{Si}-\text{O}\) gives \(\delta=0.246\), and \(\text{Al}-\text{Br}\) gives \(\delta=0.216\). Then multiply each partial charge by its bond length \(d\): \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) gives \(0.310\times131\approx41\); \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\) gives \(0.248\times214\approx53\); \(\text{Si}-\text{O}\) gives \(0.246\times163\approx40\); \(\text{Al}-\text{Br}\) gives \(0.216\times230\approx50\) (all in units of \(e\cdot\text{pm}\)). The largest product belongs to \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\) at \(\approx53\), so it carries the largest dipole moment even though it has neither the largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) nor the longest bond. The shorter, very polar \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bond and the longer, weakly polar \(\text{Al}-\text{Br}\) bond each lose because they maximize only one of the two factors that enter the product. (Choice A) The \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bond does have the largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) and therefore the largest partial charge, but its very short \(131\,\text{pm}\) bond length shrinks the product to \(\approx41\); selecting it treats partial charge as the sole determinant of the dipole moment and drops the bond-length factor that \(\mu=\delta\times d\) requires. (Choice C) The \(\text{Si}-\text{O}\) bond has a \(\Delta\text{EN}\) of \(1.54\) that is nearly tied with \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\), but its much shorter \(163\,\text{pm}\) bond gives a product of only \(\approx40\), the smallest of the four; near-equality of \(\Delta\text{EN}\) does not translate into near-equality of \(\mu\) once the differing bond lengths are included. (Choice D) The \(\text{Al}-\text{Br}\) bond is the longest at \(230\,\text{pm}\), but its smaller \(\Delta\text{EN}=1.35\) yields the smallest partial charge of the four, so its product of \(\approx50\) falls just short of \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\); choosing it treats the charge-separation distance as the sole determinant and underweights the partial-charge factor. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must convert each electronegativity difference into a partial charge, multiply that intermediate value by the corresponding bond length, and compare the four resulting products to identify the maximum.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'db7119f9-99e8-4221-bc64-f7331b51ec5b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'db7119f9-99e8-4221-bc64-f7331b51ec5b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('db7119f9-99e8-4221-bc64-f7331b51ec5b', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Maximizes partial charge (largest delta-EN) but drops the bond-length factor in mu = delta x d$a$),
  ('db7119f9-99e8-4221-bc64-f7331b51ec5b', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a$Anchors on a delta-EN near-tie with the correct bond and ignores that its much shorter bond shrinks the product$a$),
  ('db7119f9-99e8-4221-bc64-f7331b51ec5b', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Maximizes the charge-separation distance (longest bond) but underweights the smaller partial charge$a$);

-- 5. [Formal Charge · Skill 2 · ans D] id 77d3b96b-ceda-4578-906b-045eb1c19aae
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$The thiocyanate ion \(\text{SCN}^-\) has the connectivity S-C-N with carbon central, and several octet-obeying resonance contributors differ only in where the multiple bonds sit. Using the Pauling electronegativities \(\text{S}=2.58\), \(\text{C}=2.55\), and \(\text{N}=3.04\), the structure that contributes most to the resonance hybrid is most likely the form with$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"cumulated double bonds, \\(:\\!\\text{S}\\!=\\!\\text{C}\\!=\\!\\text{N}\\!:\\), giving formal charges \\(+1\\) (S), \\(0\\) (C), and \\(-2\\) (N)."},{"label":"B","text":"a single S-C bond and a C≡N triple bond, \\(:\\!\\text{S}\\!-\\!\\text{C}\\!\\equiv\\!\\text{N}\\!:\\), giving formal charges \\(-1\\) (S), \\(0\\) (C), and \\(0\\) (N)."},{"label":"C","text":"an S≡C triple bond and a single C-N bond, \\(:\\!\\text{S}\\!\\equiv\\!\\text{C}\\!-\\!\\text{N}\\!:\\), giving formal charges \\(+1\\) (S), \\(0\\) (C), and \\(-2\\) (N)."},{"label":"D","text":"cumulated double bonds, \\(:\\!\\text{S}\\!=\\!\\text{C}\\!=\\!\\text{N}\\!:\\), giving formal charges \\(0\\) (S), \\(0\\) (C), and \\(-1\\) (N)."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because, after enumerating the octet-obeying contributors and computing each formal charge from \(FC = V - (\text{nonbonding electrons}) - \tfrac{1}{2}(\text{bonding electrons})\), this form keeps every formal-charge magnitude to no more than 1 while seating the lone \(-1\) on nitrogen, the most electronegative atom present. For \(:\!\text{S}\!=\!\text{C}\!=\!\text{N}\!:\), the doubly bonded terminal S with two lone pairs has \(6-4-\tfrac{1}{2}(4)=0\); the central C with two double bonds has \(4-0-\tfrac{1}{2}(8)=0\); and the doubly bonded terminal N with two lone pairs has \(5-4-\tfrac{1}{2}(4)=-1\). A second contributor, \(:\!\text{S}\!-\!\text{C}\!\equiv\!\text{N}\!:\), also reaches the same minimal magnitude spread of \(0\), \(0\), and \(-1\), but it places the \(-1\) on sulfur. The deciding criterion is that the negative formal charge should reside on the more electronegative atom; because nitrogen (\(3.04\)) is more electronegative than sulfur (\(2.58\)), the form with the \(-1\) on nitrogen is the dominant contributor. (Choice A) The cumulated geometry actually yields magnitudes of \(0\), \(0\), and \(-1\); the listed charges of \(+1\) on sulfur and \(-2\) on nitrogen do not result from \(:\!\text{S}\!=\!\text{C}\!=\!\text{N}\!:\) and reflect a miscount of the bonding electrons in the formal-charge expression. (Choice B) This form does achieve the same low magnitudes of \(0\), \(0\), and \(-1\), but it locates the \(-1\) on sulfur rather than on the more electronegative nitrogen, so by the electronegativity criterion it is the less favorable of the two equally low-magnitude contributors and is not the major form. (Choice C) Assigning sulfur a triple bond forces a \(+1\) on sulfur and a \(-2\) on nitrogen, so its largest magnitude is 2, which is worse than the \(\pm 1\) maximum achievable, making it a minor contributor. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must enumerate the candidate structures, compute and compare formal charges across them, identify the near-tie in charge magnitude, and apply the electronegativity tie-breaker to select the governing contributor.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '77d3b96b-ceda-4578-906b-045eb1c19aae';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '77d3b96b-ceda-4578-906b-045eb1c19aae';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('77d3b96b-ceda-4578-906b-045eb1c19aae', 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Miscounts bonding electrons in FC = V - nonbonding - 1/2(bonding) for the cumulated form, producing impossible +1/-2 charges from a geometry that actually gives 0/0/-1.$a$),
  ('77d3b96b-ceda-4578-906b-045eb1c19aae', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Reaches a valid minimal-magnitude resonance form but applies the tie-breaker to the wrong atom, placing the negative charge on sulfur instead of recognizing nitrogen as more electronegative (S 2.58 vs N 3.04).$a$),
  ('77d3b96b-ceda-4578-906b-045eb1c19aae', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Drops the minimize-magnitude constraint and selects a structure carrying a -2 formal charge, treating a large charge separation as acceptable rather than disqualifying.$a$);

-- 6. [Formal Charge · Skill 2 · ans C] id 98b39d1d-1032-4293-86c5-9cb32ae70e3f
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$The cyanate ion \(\text{OCN}^-\) has the connectivity O-C-N with carbon central, and its bonding is described by several octet-satisfying resonance contributors that differ only in where the multiple bonds sit. Using the Pauling electronegativities \(\text{O}=3.44\), \(\text{C}=2.55\), and \(\text{N}=3.04\), the contributor that dominates the resonance hybrid keeps each formal-charge magnitude as small as possible while placing any negative formal charge on the most electronegative atom. That major contributor is most likely$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"the form \\(\\text{O}{\\equiv}\\text{C}{-}\\text{N}\\), giving formal charges \\(+1\\) (O), \\(0\\) (C), and \\(-2\\) (N)."},{"label":"B","text":"the form \\(\\text{O}{=}\\text{C}{=}\\text{N}\\), giving formal charges \\(0\\) (O), \\(0\\) (C), and \\(-1\\) (N)."},{"label":"C","text":"the form \\(\\text{O}{-}\\text{C}{\\equiv}\\text{N}\\), giving formal charges \\(-1\\) (O), \\(0\\) (C), and \\(0\\) (N)."},{"label":"D","text":"the form \\(\\text{O}{-}\\text{C}{\\equiv}\\text{N}\\), giving formal charges \\(0\\) (O), \\(0\\) (C), and \\(-1\\) (N)."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because that arrangement holds every formal-charge magnitude to no more than 1 while seating the \(-1\) on oxygen, the most electronegative atom. With 16 valence electrons (\(6+4+5+1\)) and carbon always central with four bonds and no lone pair (\(\text{FC}_\text{C}=4-0-\tfrac{1}{2}(8)=0\)), three octet-valid contributors exist. Using \(\text{FC}=V-(\text{nonbonding }e^-)-\tfrac{1}{2}(\text{bonding }e^-)\): the form \(\text{O}{-}\text{C}{\equiv}\text{N}\) gives O \(6-6-\tfrac{1}{2}(2)=-1\) and N \(5-2-\tfrac{1}{2}(6)=0\); the form \(\text{O}{=}\text{C}{=}\text{N}\) gives O \(6-4-\tfrac{1}{2}(4)=0\) and N \(5-4-\tfrac{1}{2}(4)=-1\); and the form \(\text{O}{\equiv}\text{C}{-}\text{N}\) gives O \(6-2-\tfrac{1}{2}(6)=+1\) and N \(5-6-\tfrac{1}{2}(2)=-2\). Applying the magnitude rule first, the \(\text{O}{\equiv}\text{C}{-}\text{N}\) form (\(+1, 0, -2\)) is rejected for its magnitude-2 charge, leaving \(\text{O}{-}\text{C}{\equiv}\text{N}\) (\(-1, 0, 0\)) and \(\text{O}{=}\text{C}{=}\text{N}\) (\(0, 0, -1\)) tied at a single unit of charge each. The tie is broken by the rule that the negative formal charge belongs on the more electronegative atom: oxygen (\(3.44\)) exceeds nitrogen (\(3.04\)), so \(\text{O}{-}\text{C}{\equiv}\text{N}\), which places the \(-1\) on oxygen, is the major contributor. (Choice A) The \(\text{O}{\equiv}\text{C}{-}\text{N}\) form places a \(-2\) on nitrogen and a \(+1\) on oxygen, so its largest formal-charge magnitude is 2 and it puts positive charge on the most electronegative atom; both defects make it a minor contributor rather than the dominant one. (Choice B) This cumulated form has the correct minimal magnitudes (\(0, 0, -1\)) but locates the \(-1\) on nitrogen rather than on the more electronegative oxygen, so by the electronegativity criterion it is the less favorable of the two equally low-magnitude contributors. (Choice D) This pairs the correct keyed structure \(\text{O}{-}\text{C}{\equiv}\text{N}\) with an incorrect charge assignment of \(0\) on oxygen and \(-1\) on nitrogen; in that structure oxygen has one bond and three lone pairs (\(6-6-1=-1\)) while the triple-bonded nitrogen is \(0\) (\(5-2-3=0\)), so the charges are swapped relative to the actual values. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must enumerate competing octet-valid structures, compute formal charges across all atoms in each, and chain the magnitude-minimization and electronegativity rules to select which contributor governs.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '98b39d1d-1032-4293-86c5-9cb32ae70e3f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '98b39d1d-1032-4293-86c5-9cb32ae70e3f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('98b39d1d-1032-4293-86c5-9cb32ae70e3f', 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Selecting a structure with an unnecessarily large formal-charge magnitude (-2) that also places positive charge on the most electronegative atom$a$),
  ('98b39d1d-1032-4293-86c5-9cb32ae70e3f', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Picking the form with the correct minimal magnitudes but the negative charge on the less electronegative atom (nitrogen instead of oxygen)$a$),
  ('98b39d1d-1032-4293-86c5-9cb32ae70e3f', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Pairing the correct keyed structure with formal charges swapped between oxygen and nitrogen$a$);

-- 7. [Formal Charge · Skill 2 · ans B] id 9c146153-1a72-4d6f-805a-e0759baa7f23
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist starts the Lewis structures of perchlorate \(\text{ClO}_4^-\) and sulfate \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) by drawing each central atom with four single bonds to oxygen and no lone pair on the central atom, then rebuilds each into its lowest-formal-charge structure by converting single bonds to terminal double bonds wherever doing so reduces formal-charge magnitudes. In those lowest-formal-charge structures, the formal charges on the central chlorine and the central sulfur, respectively, are most likely$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+3\\) on Cl and \\(+2\\) on S, because the central atom keeps its four single bonds and only the terminal oxygens are rearranged, leaving the central formal charges at their all-single-bond values."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0\\) on Cl and \\(0\\) on S, because the lowest-formal-charge structure of \\(\\text{ClO}_4^-\\) needs three \\(\\text{Cl=O}\\) bonds while that of \\(\\text{SO}_4^{2-}\\) needs two \\(\\text{S=O}\\) bonds, driving each central atom to zero."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+2\\) on Cl and \\(+1\\) on S, because converting one single bond to one terminal double bond in each ion lowers each central formal charge by exactly one unit from its all-single-bond value."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+7\\) on Cl and \\(+6\\) on S, because the lowest-formal-charge structure assigns every shared bonding pair to the more electronegative oxygen atoms."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because formal charge is computed as \(\text{FC} = (\text{valence electrons}) - (\text{nonbonding electrons}) - \tfrac{1}{2}(\text{bonding electrons})\), and the lowest-formal-charge structure is found by adding terminal double bonds until the central atom's formal charge is minimized. In the all-single-bond starting structure, chlorine (\(7\) valence electrons, no lone pair, four single bonds, \(8\) bonding electrons) has \(\text{FC} = 7 - 0 - \tfrac{1}{2}(8) = +3\), and sulfur (\(6\) valence electrons, four single bonds) has \(\text{FC} = 6 - 0 - \tfrac{1}{2}(8) = +2\). Each single-to-double conversion adds one shared pair at the central atom and so lowers its formal charge by \(\tfrac{1}{2}(2) = 1\), at the cost of an expanded octet that period-3 atoms can accommodate. Driving chlorine from \(+3\) to \(0\) therefore requires three \(\text{Cl=O}\) double bonds (seven bonding pairs total): \(\text{FC} = 7 - 0 - \tfrac{1}{2}(14) = 0\); the three doubly bonded oxygens are each \(0\) and the one single-bonded oxygen is \(-1\), summing to the ion's \(-1\). Driving sulfur from \(+2\) to \(0\) requires two \(\text{S=O}\) double bonds (six bonding pairs total): \(\text{FC} = 6 - 0 - \tfrac{1}{2}(12) = 0\); the two doubly bonded oxygens are each \(0\) and the two single-bonded oxygens are each \(-1\), summing to the ion's \(-2\). Both central atoms reach \(0\), but only because the two ions require a different number of double bonds, set by their differing all-single-bond starting values. (Choice A) This stops at the all-single-bond structure and merely shuffles the terminal oxygens; in fact the lowest-formal-charge criterion converts single bonds at the central atom to double bonds, and each added shared pair lowers the central formal charge, so neither center remains at \(+3\) or \(+2\). (Choice C) This applies only one single-to-double conversion to each ion, lowering chlorine to \(+2\) and sulfur to \(+1\); the minimization is incomplete, because chlorine still permits two more \(\text{Cl=O}\) bonds and sulfur one more \(\text{S=O}\) bond before each central formal charge reaches its minimum of \(0\). (Choice D) The values \(+7\) and \(+6\) are the oxidation numbers of chlorine and sulfur, obtained by assigning all shared electrons to the more electronegative oxygens; formal charge instead splits each bonding pair evenly, so the lowest-formal-charge structures leave both central atoms at \(0\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the formal-charge formula to two different central atoms, determine how many terminal double bonds the lowest-formal-charge criterion requires for each, and recognize that distinct bond counts can still drive both central formal charges to the same value.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '9c146153-1a72-4d6f-805a-e0759baa7f23';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9c146153-1a72-4d6f-805a-e0759baa7f23';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9c146153-1a72-4d6f-805a-e0759baa7f23', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Computes the all-single-bond central formal charges (+3 for Cl, +2 for S) correctly but drops the single-to-double bond conversions that the lowest-formal-charge criterion requires, leaving the central atoms at their unminimized starting values.$a$),
  ('9c146153-1a72-4d6f-805a-e0759baa7f23', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Applies only one single-to-double conversion to each ion, halting the formal-charge minimization prematurely and reporting +2 for Cl and +1 for S instead of continuing to the true minimum of 0.$a$),
  ('9c146153-1a72-4d6f-805a-e0759baa7f23', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Confuses formal charge with oxidation number, reporting the +7 and +6 oxidation states of chlorine and sulfur by assigning all shared electrons to oxygen rather than splitting each bonding pair evenly.$a$);

-- 8. [Intermolecular Forces · Skill 2 · ans B] id 7206ea0a-9f5c-475a-9ede-6f56c61e5eae
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Four liquids are compared at \(1\,\text{atm}\): 1-propanol \((\text{CH}_3\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH})\), octane \((\text{C}_8\text{H}_{18})\), dimethyl ether \((\text{CH}_3\text{OCH}_3)\), and propane \((\text{C}_3\text{H}_8)\). Each molecule's dominant intermolecular force must be identified before its boiling point can be ranked. Which of these liquids most likely has the highest normal boiling point?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"1-Propanol, because hydrogen bonding is always a stronger intermolecular attraction than the London dispersion forces of a nonpolar molecule, regardless of molecular size."},{"label":"B","text":"Octane, because its large, highly polarizable eight-carbon chain generates cumulative London dispersion forces strong enough to surpass the hydrogen bonding in the much smaller 1-propanol."},{"label":"C","text":"Dimethyl ether, because its permanent dipole produces dipole-dipole attraction, and a polar molecule must boil higher than any nonpolar molecule present."},{"label":"D","text":"Propane, because as the smallest, most compact nonpolar molecule its electron cloud is the most tightly held and therefore the most strongly attracted to its neighbors."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the comparison must be made in three chained steps. First, assign each molecule's dominant intermolecular force: 1-propanol has an \(\text{O}-\text{H}\) group, so it hydrogen bonds; dimethyl ether is polar but has no \(\text{H}\) on \(\text{N}\), \(\text{O}\), or \(\text{F}\), so it relies on dipole-dipole forces; octane and propane are nonpolar hydrocarbons that have only London dispersion forces. Second, recall that the per-interaction strength heuristic ranks hydrogen bonding > dipole-dipole > dispersion, which would naively favor 1-propanol. Third, recognize the controlling constraint: dispersion forces scale with molecular size and polarizability, and octane \((\text{C}_8\text{H}_{18},\ \approx 114\ \text{g/mol})\) has a far larger, more polarizable electron cloud and much greater contact surface area than 1-propanol \((\text{C}_3,\ \approx 60\ \text{g/mol})\). The accumulated dispersion attraction across octane's long chain outweighs the single hydrogen-bonding network of the small alcohol, so octane boils highest (octane \(\approx 126\,^\circ\text{C}\) versus 1-propanol \(\approx 97\,^\circ\text{C}\)). The decisive factor is therefore size-driven dispersion overtaking a stronger per-pair force, not the per-pair force ranking alone. (Choice A) The claim that hydrogen bonding always exceeds dispersion ignores that dispersion is additive and grows with chain length; for a small alcohol such as 1-propanol the hydrogen-bond network is outpaced by the dispersion forces of a sufficiently large hydrocarbon, so this drops the size constraint. (Choice C) Dimethyl ether's permanent dipole gives only dipole-dipole attraction, and its small size \((\approx 46\ \text{g/mol})\) makes its total intermolecular attraction weak; a permanent dipole does not guarantee a higher boiling point than a much larger nonpolar molecule, so polarity alone does not override dispersion here. (Choice D) Propane is the smallest of the nonpolar candidates and therefore has the least polarizable electron cloud and the weakest dispersion forces, giving it the lowest boiling point of the four; the reasoning reverses the size-polarizability relationship, since compactness weakens, not strengthens, dispersion. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the identification of each dominant intermolecular force with the way dispersion magnitude scales with molecular size to determine which force ultimately governs.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '7206ea0a-9f5c-475a-9ede-6f56c61e5eae';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7206ea0a-9f5c-475a-9ede-6f56c61e5eae';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7206ea0a-9f5c-475a-9ede-6f56c61e5eae', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Applies the rigid per-interaction strength ranking (hydrogen bonding always beats dispersion) and drops the controlling constraint that dispersion accumulates with molecular size, so the large nonpolar chain is wrongly assumed weaker than the small alcohol.$a$),
  ('7206ea0a-9f5c-475a-9ede-6f56c61e5eae', 'C', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly identifies dimethyl ether as polar with dipole-dipole forces but over-generalizes to 'polar always boils higher than nonpolar,' ignoring that its small molar mass gives weak total attraction relative to a much larger nonpolar molecule.$a$),
  ('7206ea0a-9f5c-475a-9ede-6f56c61e5eae', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the size-polarizability-dispersion relationship, treating the smallest, most compact molecule as having the strongest dispersion when in fact larger, more polarizable electron clouds produce stronger dispersion.$a$);

-- 9. [Intermolecular Forces · Skill 2 · ans A] id e17da915-127e-4272-8a98-82a97b724522
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A rigid flask is filled exactly to its brim with \(36.0\ \text{g}\) of liquid \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) (density \(\approx 1.00\ \text{g/cm}^3\)), leaving no headspace, and the open flask is then cooled until the sample freezes completely to ice (density \(\approx 0.92\ \text{g/cm}^3\)). Hydrogen bonding dominates the intermolecular interactions in both phases. The volume of frozen \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) that most likely overflows the flask is closest to$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(3.1\\ \\text{cm}^3\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(2.9\\ \\text{cm}^3\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(39\\ \\text{cm}^3\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0\\ \\text{cm}^3\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the calculation must be chained from the intermolecular behavior of water. Freezing conserves the \(36.0\ \text{g}\) of \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), but the extensive hydrogen bonding in ice locks the molecules into a more open arrangement than the close-packed liquid; this larger spacing is precisely why the solid has the lower density. Because the same mass now occupies a lower-density solid, the volume must grow, and the difference is forced out of the brim-full flask. Computing each phase volume from \(V = m/\rho\): the liquid occupies \(V_{\text{liquid}} = 36.0/1.00 = 36.0\ \text{cm}^3\) (the flask's capacity), while the ice occupies \(V_{\text{ice}} = 36.0/0.92 \approx 39.1\ \text{cm}^3\). The overflow is the excess that no longer fits, \(V_{\text{ice}} - V_{\text{liquid}} = 39.1 - 36.0 \approx 3.1\ \text{cm}^3\). (Choice B) A value near \(2.9\ \text{cm}^3\) results from taking the fractional density drop \(1 - \rho_{\text{ice}}/\rho_{\text{liquid}} = 1 - 0.92/1.00 = 0.08\) and applying it directly to the original \(36.0\ \text{cm}^3\) (\(0.08 \times 36.0 = 2.88\)); this uses the wrong ratio, since for a fixed mass the volume scales as \(1/\rho\), so the expansion factor is \(\rho_{\text{liquid}}/\rho_{\text{ice}} = 1.087\) applied to the original volume, not the density-drop fraction. (Choice C) A value near \(39\ \text{cm}^3\) is the total volume of the ice, \(36.0/0.92\), reported without subtracting the \(36.0\ \text{cm}^3\) that still fits inside the flask; only the excess beyond the flask's capacity can overflow. (Choice D) An overflow of zero treats the sealed-mass condition as a fixed-volume condition; conserving mass does not forbid a volume change, and the open hydrogen-bonded lattice specifically forces the conserved mass to expand, so some volume must spill from the brim-full flask. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must recognize that hydrogen bonding produces the open, lower-density solid lattice that drives expansion, then chain the two densities through \(V = m/\rho\) for each phase and take their difference to isolate the overflow.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'e17da915-127e-4272-8a98-82a97b724522';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e17da915-127e-4272-8a98-82a97b724522';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e17da915-127e-4272-8a98-82a97b724522', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Multiplies the fractional density drop (1 - rho_ice/rho_liq = 0.08) by the original 36.0 cm^3 to get 2.88 cm^3, using the wrong ratio instead of the 1/rho scaling that gives the m/rho_ice - m/rho_liq difference.$a$),
  ('e17da915-127e-4272-8a98-82a97b724522', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Computes the full ice volume V_ice = 36.0/0.92 = 39 cm^3 but forgets the final subtraction of the 36.0 cm^3 that still fits in the flask, reporting total ice volume rather than the overflow.$a$),
  ('e17da915-127e-4272-8a98-82a97b724522', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Confuses mass conservation with volume conservation, concluding a sealed fixed-mass sample cannot change volume and dropping the open-lattice expansion entirely.$a$);

-- 10. [Ionic Character & Bond Type · Skill 2 · ans C] id 275ab843-f9fe-4bdd-a07e-41281995c68b
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A gas-phase diatomic \(\text{M}{-}\text{X}\) has a measured dipole moment of \(4.80\ \text{D}\) and a bond length of \(250\ \text{pm}\). Treating a fully ionic \(\text{M}^+\text{X}^-\) bond as two point charges of magnitude \(e = 1.602 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{C}\) separated by the bond length (with \(1\ \text{D} = 3.336 \times 10^{-30}\ \text{C}\cdot\text{m}\)), and taking a bond to be ionic only when its percent ionic character reaches at least \(50\%\), the bond is most likely best classified as$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"polar covalent, because the percent ionic character is about \\(20\\%\\), which falls below the \\(50\\%\\) ionic cutoff"},{"label":"B","text":"ionic, because the percent ionic character is about \\(80\\%\\), which exceeds the \\(50\\%\\) ionic cutoff"},{"label":"C","text":"polar covalent, because the percent ionic character is about \\(40\\%\\), which falls below the \\(50\\%\\) ionic cutoff"},{"label":"D","text":"ionic, because a measured dipole moment of \\(4.80\\ \\text{D}\\) is large, placing the bond above the \\(50\\%\\) ionic cutoff"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because percent ionic character is the measured dipole moment divided by the dipole a fully ionic bond would have, and that calculation places the bond below the stated ionic cutoff. First, the hypothetical fully ionic dipole is \(\mu_{\text{ionic}} = e \times d = (1.602 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{C})(250 \times 10^{-12}\ \text{m}) = 4.005 \times 10^{-29}\ \text{C}\cdot\text{m}\); converting, \(\mu_{\text{ionic}} = (4.005 \times 10^{-29})/(3.336 \times 10^{-30}) \approx 12.0\ \text{D}\). Next, percent ionic character \(= (\mu_{\text{measured}}/\mu_{\text{ionic}}) \times 100 = (4.80/12.0) \times 100 \approx 40\%\). Finally, because \(40\%\) is below the \(50\%\) threshold given in the stem, the shared pair is unequally distributed rather than transferred, so the bond is classified as polar covalent rather than ionic. (Choice A) A value of \(20\%\) results from using a charge of \(2e\) (a full bonding pair) rather than the single unit charge \(e\) appropriate to a singly charged \(\text{M}^+\text{X}^-\) pair, which doubles \(\mu_{\text{ionic}}\) to \(24.0\ \text{D}\) and halves the ratio; only one unit of charge is separated in the fully ionic limit of this bond, so \(20\%\) understates the ionic character even though it happens to land on the correct side of the cutoff. (Choice B) A value of \(80\%\) results from using the atomic radius, \(d/2 = 125\ \text{pm}\), in place of the full bond length, which halves \(\mu_{\text{ionic}}\) to \(6.0\ \text{D}\) and doubles the computed ionic character; the dipole separation is the full internuclear distance, not the radius, so \(80\%\) overstates the charge transfer. (Choice D) Judging the bond from the raw \(4.80\ \text{D}\) dipole alone ignores that a large dipole can arise from a long bond carrying only modest charge separation; the percent ionic character, not the absolute dipole, must be compared to the threshold, and that calculation yields \(40\%\), below the cutoff. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a unit-converted dipole calculation into a ratio and then apply a stated threshold to reach the correct bond-type classification.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '275ab843-f9fe-4bdd-a07e-41281995c68b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '275ab843-f9fe-4bdd-a07e-41281995c68b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('275ab843-f9fe-4bdd-a07e-41281995c68b', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Uses a charge of 2e (a full bonding electron pair) rather than the single unit charge e for a singly charged M+X- pair, doubling the ionic dipole to 24.0 D and halving the ratio to 20%; reaches the correct 'polar covalent' verdict by incorrect reasoning.$a$),
  ('275ab843-f9fe-4bdd-a07e-41281995c68b', 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Uses the atomic radius (d/2 = 125 pm) instead of the full bond length (250 pm) for the charge separation, halving the fully-ionic dipole to 6.0 D and doubling percent ionic character to 80%, flipping the classification to ionic.$a$),
  ('275ab843-f9fe-4bdd-a07e-41281995c68b', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Drops the controlling constraint and judges bond type from the raw measured dipole (4.80 D looks large) instead of computing percent ionic character relative to the fully-ionic dipole, ignoring that the long 250 pm bond inflates the dipole at modest charge separation.$a$);

-- 11. [Lewis Structures · Skill 2 · ans B] id 2ea5be05-3978-4f23-bab1-346da5f8fd90
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Isocyanic acid \(\text{HNCO}\) (connectivity H-N-C-O) is drawn as the neutral, octet-satisfying structure (duet for H) that also drives every formal charge to zero. In that structure, how many bonding pairs and how many lone (nonbonding) pairs are present in the whole molecule?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"5 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs"},{"label":"B","text":"5 bonding pairs and 3 lone pairs"},{"label":"C","text":"4 bonding pairs and 4 lone pairs"},{"label":"D","text":"3 bonding pairs and 5 lone pairs"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the molecule supplies \(1 + 5 + 4 + 6 = 16\) valence electrons (eight pairs), and the only way to give nitrogen, carbon, and oxygen full octets while keeping every formal charge at zero is the cumulated-double-bond skeleton \(\text{H}-\text{N}=\text{C}=\text{O}\). In that structure nitrogen forms one \(\text{N}-\text{H}\) single bond and one \(\text{N}=\text{C}\) double bond and retains one lone pair (formal charge \(5 - 2 - 3 = 0\)); carbon forms the \(\text{N}=\text{C}\) and \(\text{C}=\text{O}\) double bonds for four shared pairs and no lone pairs (formal charge \(4 - 0 - 4 = 0\)); oxygen forms the \(\text{C}=\text{O}\) double bond and keeps two lone pairs (formal charge \(6 - 4 - 2 = 0\)). Counting shared pairs gives the \(\text{N}-\text{H}\) bond (\(1\)) plus the \(\text{N}=\text{C}\) bond (\(2\)) plus the \(\text{C}=\text{O}\) bond (\(2\)), for \(5\) bonding pairs; the lone pairs are one on nitrogen and two on oxygen, for \(3\) lone pairs. The tally \(2(5) + 2(3) = 16\) electrons conserves the full valence count. (Choice A) This keeps the correct five shared pairs but drops nitrogen's lone pair; after nitrogen forms three bonds it has used six of its five-plus-shared electrons, and two nonbonding electrons must remain on nitrogen to complete its octet, so omitting them accounts for only \(14\) of the \(16\) electrons. (Choice C) This results from placing only one of the two required double bonds, for example \(\text{H}-\text{N}=\text{C}-\text{O}\), which leaves the central carbon with only three bonds and six electrons; that arrangement violates carbon's octet, so it cannot be the octet-satisfying structure even though its \(4 + 4 = 8\) pairs conserve the electron count. (Choice D) This treats each atom-atom connection as a single shared pair, counting \(\text{N}-\text{H}\), \(\text{N}-\text{C}\), and \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) as three bonding pairs and reassigning the remaining electrons as five lone pairs; it ignores that both the \(\text{N}=\text{C}\) and \(\text{C}=\text{O}\) linkages are double bonds worth two shared pairs each. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the valence-electron count, the octet rule, and the lowest-formal-charge criterion to fix the bonding skeleton before the bonding and lone pairs can be tallied.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '2ea5be05-3978-4f23-bab1-346da5f8fd90';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2ea5be05-3978-4f23-bab1-346da5f8fd90';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2ea5be05-3978-4f23-bab1-346da5f8fd90', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Correct bonding-pair count but drops the central nitrogen's lone pair, undercounting nonbonding electrons by one pair$a$),
  ('2ea5be05-3978-4f23-bab1-346da5f8fd90', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Places only one of the two required double bonds, leaving carbon with an incomplete octet while still appearing to conserve 16 electrons$a$),
  ('2ea5be05-3978-4f23-bab1-346da5f8fd90', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Counts each atom-atom connection as a single shared pair, ignoring that the N=C and C=O linkages are double bonds worth two pairs each$a$);

-- 12. [Lewis Structures · Skill 2 · ans D] id bf487459-acde-42d4-9759-b15d13680243
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A student proposes a Lewis structure for the chlorite ion \(\text{ClO}_2^-\) (connectivity O-Cl-O) in which chlorine forms one \(\text{Cl}-\text{O}\) single bond to each oxygen, every oxygen carries three lone pairs, and the central chlorine carries three lone pairs. What is the most defensible objection to this proposed structure?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"It draws only \\(18\\) electrons, leaving chlorine electron-deficient, so an additional lone pair must be added to chlorine."},{"label":"B","text":"The structure is impossible because the available electrons total \\(19\\), an odd number that leaves an unpaired electron on chlorine."},{"label":"C","text":"It is acceptable, because chlorine is a period-3 atom and the ten electrons around it are an allowed expanded octet."},{"label":"D","text":"It draws \\(22\\) electrons, but \\(\\text{ClO}_2^-\\) supplies only \\(20\\), so one chlorine lone pair must be removed."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because a valid Lewis structure can never draw more electrons than the atoms actually supply. First tally the available valence electrons, remembering to add one for the \(-1\) charge: \(7 + 2(6) + 1 = 20\). Next count what the proposed structure actually draws: two \(\text{Cl}-\text{O}\) single bonds contribute \(4\) electrons, the two terminal oxygens each carry three lone pairs for \(2(6) = 12\) electrons, and the central chlorine carries three lone pairs for \(6\) electrons, giving \(4 + 12 + 6 = 22\) drawn electrons. Because \(22 > 20\), the structure over-counts by exactly one pair, so one of chlorine's lone pairs is illegitimate and must be deleted; the corrected structure leaves chlorine with two lone pairs. (Choice A) Reporting \(18\) electrons and electron deficiency reverses the actual error: the proposed structure has too many electrons, not too few, and adding a further lone pair would push the total to \(24\), worsening the over-count rather than repairing it. (Choice B) Tallying \(7 + 2(6) = 19\) drops the electron contributed by the ion's \(-1\) charge; the true total is \(20\), an even number that pairs completely, so \(\text{ClO}_2^-\) is not a radical and is a perfectly stable closed-shell ion. (Choice C) Although chlorine is a period-3 element that can hold more than eight electrons in species such as \(\text{ClF}_3\), the surrounding count of \(2(2) + 6 = 10\) electrons is not the governing flaw here; the structure is disqualified at the prior step because its total of \(22\) drawn electrons exceeds the \(20\) the ion provides, so no appeal to octet expansion can rescue it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a charge-inclusive valence-electron tally with an electron count of the drawn structure and then identify which discrepancy actually invalidates it.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'bf487459-acde-42d4-9759-b15d13680243';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'bf487459-acde-42d4-9759-b15d13680243';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('bf487459-acde-42d4-9759-b15d13680243', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the direction of the counting error, treating an over-count as an under-count and prescribing the addition of electrons when removal is required.$a$),
  ('bf487459-acde-42d4-9759-b15d13680243', 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Omits the electron from the ionic charge, tallying 19 instead of 20, then misreads the resulting odd count as evidence of an impossible radical species.$a$),
  ('bf487459-acde-42d4-9759-b15d13680243', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a$Invokes the legitimate period-3 octet-expansion rule as a blanket justification, failing to notice that the total electron count already disqualifies the structure before octet expansion is even relevant.$a$);

-- 13. [Molecular Polarity · Skill 2 · ans A] id 184a7f92-40cc-471f-b6ba-30f737abe84a
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Each of the molecules \(\text{SiF}_4\), \(\text{XeF}_4\), \(\text{SF}_4\), and \(\text{BF}_3\) is built only from highly polar bonds to fluorine, yet only one of them has a nonzero net molecular dipole moment. Once the lone pairs on each central atom are accounted for, the molecule most likely to be polar is$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{SF}_4\\), because its single equatorial lone pair gives a seesaw shape in which the \\(\\text{S}-\\text{F}\\) bond dipoles fail to cancel."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{XeF}_4\\), because the two lone pairs on xenon distort the molecule and prevent the four \\(\\text{Xe}-\\text{F}\\) dipoles from canceling."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{SiF}_4\\), because its four strongly polar \\(\\text{Si}-\\text{F}\\) bonds give the molecule a large permanent dipole."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{BF}_3\\), because the lone pair on boron forces a trigonal-pyramidal shape whose \\(\\text{B}-\\text{F}\\) dipoles do not cancel."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because deciding polarity requires chaining two separate steps for each species: first fix the electron-domain geometry from a lone-pair count, then test whether the individually polar bond dipoles cancel by symmetry. \(\text{SF}_4\) has a central sulfur with \(6 + 4(7) = 34\) valence electrons; after four \(\text{S}-\text{F}\) bonds and three lone pairs on each fluorine, two electrons remain on sulfur as one lone pair, giving an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_1\) seesaw shape. That single equatorial lone pair occupies one site of the trigonal-bipyramidal arrangement, so the four \(\text{S}-\text{F}\) bond dipoles point in directions that do not balance, and their vector sum is nonzero. Each \(\text{S}-\text{F}\) bond is polar (\(\text{F}\) far more electronegative than \(\text{S}\)), and because the geometry is asymmetric these dipoles add to a net molecular dipole, exactly the same survival-of-bond-dipoles principle that makes \(\text{CHCl}_3\) polar while \(\text{CCl}_4\) is not. (Choice B) \(\text{XeF}_4\) is \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_2\): xenon keeps two lone pairs, but they occupy the two axial positions of an octahedral electron-domain set, leaving the four \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) bonds coplanar in a square-planar arrangement; the two trans lone pairs cancel each other and the four equal in-plane dipoles cancel pairwise, so the molecule is nonpolar despite having two lone pairs. (Choice C) \(\text{SiF}_4\) is \(\text{AX}_4\) with no lone pairs on silicon, a regular tetrahedron of four identical \(\text{Si}-\text{F}\) bonds; despite each bond being strongly polar, the four equal dipoles point to the vertices of a tetrahedron and cancel exactly, so the molecule is nonpolar, and "polar bonds" alone do not make a polar molecule. (Choice D) \(\text{BF}_3\) has only \(3 + 3(7) = 24\) valence electrons, all consumed by three \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bonds and the fluorine lone pairs, leaving boron with zero lone pairs as an \(\text{AX}_3\) trigonal-planar molecule, not trigonal pyramidal; its three equal dipoles at \(120^\circ\) cancel, so it is nonpolar, and the choice mistakenly imports the pyramidal geometry of an \(\text{AX}_3\text{E}_1\) species such as \(\text{NF}_3\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must determine each molecule's electron-domain geometry from its lone-pair count and then evaluate whether its symmetric or asymmetric bond-dipole arrangement leaves a net molecular dipole.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '184a7f92-40cc-471f-b6ba-30f737abe84a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '184a7f92-40cc-471f-b6ba-30f737abe84a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('184a7f92-40cc-471f-b6ba-30f737abe84a', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly notes XeF4 has two lone pairs but wrongly concludes they break the symmetry, missing that the trans axial lone pairs and square-planar bonds cancel.$a$),
  ('184a7f92-40cc-471f-b6ba-30f737abe84a', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Assumes any molecule made of polar bonds must itself be polar, ignoring symmetric cancellation in the tetrahedral AX4 geometry of SiF4.$a$),
  ('184a7f92-40cc-471f-b6ba-30f737abe84a', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Miscounts boron's electron domains and treats BF3 as trigonal pyramidal (AX3E1, like NF3) rather than trigonal planar (AX3), inventing a lone pair on boron.$a$);

-- 14. [Molecular Polarity · Skill 2 · ans B] id ba2586a3-de14-4350-907f-bc5c4046629e
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Ammonia \( \text{NH}_3 \) and nitrogen trifluoride \( \text{NF}_3 \) are both trigonal-pyramidal molecules built on a central nitrogen that retains one lone pair, and each contains three equivalent polar bonds. Using the electronegativities \( \text{H} = 2.1 \), \( \text{N} = 3.0 \), and \( \text{F} = 4.0 \), which molecule most likely has the larger net molecular dipole moment, and why?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\( \\text{NF}_3 \\), because each \\( \\text{N}{-}\\text{F} \\) bond is more polar than \\( \\text{N}{-}\\text{H} \\) \\((\\Delta\\text{EN} = 1.0\\ \\text{vs.}\\ 0.9)\\), and the larger bond dipoles must give the larger molecular dipole."},{"label":"B","text":"\\( \\text{NH}_3 \\), because each \\( \\text{N}{-}\\text{H} \\) bond dipole points toward nitrogen, so the resultant of the three bond dipoles reinforces the lone-pair dipole along the same axis."},{"label":"C","text":"\\( \\text{NF}_3 \\), because each \\( \\text{N}{-}\\text{F} \\) bond dipole points toward fluorine, so the resultant of the three bond dipoles reinforces the lone-pair dipole along the same axis."},{"label":"D","text":"\\( \\text{NH}_3 \\), because its bent geometry leaves the three \\( \\text{N}{-}\\text{H} \\) bond dipoles unable to cancel, whereas the symmetric pyramid of \\( \\text{NF}_3 \\) cancels its bond dipoles."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the net molecular dipole is the vector sum of the bond dipoles and the lone-pair contribution, and that sum depends on the direction each bond dipole points, not merely on how polar each bond is. Both molecules are AX\(_3\)E (tetrahedral electron-pair geometry, trigonal-pyramidal molecular geometry), so geometry alone cannot distinguish them; the discriminator is direction. In \( \text{NH}_3 \), nitrogen \((\text{EN} = 3.0)\) is more electronegative than hydrogen \((2.1)\), so each \( \text{N}{-}\text{H} \) bond dipole points from H toward N, i.e. up the C\(_3\) axis on the same side as the nitrogen lone pair; the resultant of the three bond dipoles and the lone-pair dipole therefore point the same way and ADD, giving a large net dipole (measured \(\approx 1.47\ \text{D}\)). In \( \text{NF}_3 \), fluorine \((4.0)\) is more electronegative than nitrogen \((3.0)\), so each \( \text{N}{-}\text{F} \) bond dipole points from N toward F, i.e. down toward the fluorine base and OPPOSITE the lone-pair dipole; the two contributions partially cancel, leaving only a small net dipole (measured \(\approx 0.24\ \text{D}\)). Thus \( \text{NF}_3 \) has the more polar individual bonds yet the smaller molecular dipole, because in \( \text{NH}_3 \) the bond dipoles and lone-pair dipole reinforce while in \( \text{NF}_3 \) they oppose. (Choice A) is incorrect because comparing only bond-dipole magnitude ignores direction; although \( \text{N}{-}\text{F} \) (\(\Delta\text{EN} = 1.0\)) is individually more polar than \( \text{N}{-}\text{H} \) (\(\Delta\text{EN} = 0.9\)), the \( \text{N}{-}\text{F} \) bond dipoles oppose the lone-pair dipole and partially cancel, so larger bonds give the smaller molecular dipole. (Choice C) correctly identifies the direction of the \( \text{N}{-}\text{F} \) bond dipoles (toward fluorine) but draws the wrong conclusion: those dipoles point away from the lone pair and oppose it rather than reinforce it, which reduces the net dipole rather than enlarging it. (Choice D) is incorrect because \( \text{NH}_3 \) is not bent and \( \text{NF}_3 \) is not symmetric enough to cancel; both are trigonal pyramidal, and in a pyramid the three bond dipoles never fully cancel, so the cancellation premise is false for both molecules. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain VSEPR geometry, bond-dipole direction inferred from electronegativity, and vector addition with the lone-pair dipole to decide which reinforcement-versus-opposition outcome governs the net molecular dipole.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'ba2586a3-de14-4350-907f-bc5c4046629e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ba2586a3-de14-4350-907f-bc5c4046629e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ba2586a3-de14-4350-907f-bc5c4046629e', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Equates larger individual bond-dipole magnitude (greater dEN) with larger molecular dipole, ignoring vector direction and the lone-pair contribution$a$),
  ('ba2586a3-de14-4350-907f-bc5c4046629e', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Gets the N-F dipole direction right but reverses the reinforce/oppose conclusion, claiming opposed vectors reinforce the lone pair$a$),
  ('ba2586a3-de14-4350-907f-bc5c4046629e', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a$Misapplies the symmetry-cancellation rule by wrongly calling NH3 bent and treating NF3 as a symmetric (cancelling) shape$a$);

-- 15. [Octet Rule & Exceptions · Skill 2 · ans D] id 38d17142-e29b-4d2d-adc3-e1a44021dcc4
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Gaseous \(\text{BeCl}_2\) is a linear monomer in which beryllium completes two \(\text{Be-Cl}\) single bonds, but on cooling it condenses to a solid in which each beryllium attains a full octet by accepting lone pairs from neighboring chloride centers. Starting from the electron count around beryllium in the vapor-phase monomer, the solid most likely achieves the octet by having each beryllium$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"accept three additional lone pairs, raising it from four to ten surrounding electrons through an expanded octet and giving five bonds in a trigonal-bipyramidal arrangement."},{"label":"B","text":"accept one additional lone pair, raising it from six to eight surrounding electrons and giving three bonds in a trigonal-planar arrangement (~\\(120^\\circ\\))."},{"label":"C","text":"accept one additional lone pair, raising it from four to six surrounding electrons and giving three bonds in a bent arrangement (~\\(120^\\circ\\))."},{"label":"D","text":"accept two additional lone pairs, raising it from four to eight surrounding electrons and giving four bonds in a tetrahedral arrangement (~\\(109.5^\\circ\\))."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the reasoning must be chained in three steps. First, count the electrons around beryllium in the vapor-phase monomer: beryllium contributes its two valence electrons and each chlorine contributes one to its bond, giving two \(\text{Be-Cl}\) bonding pairs, so just \(2 \times 2 = 4\) electrons surround beryllium, the defining electron-deficient (quartet) state. Second, determine the shortfall: an octet requires eight electrons, so \(8 - 4 = 4\) more electrons are needed, which is \(4 \div 2 = 2\) additional electron pairs. Third, translate those two accepted pairs into structure: because the terminal chlorines must supply these as lone pairs through coordinate (dative) bonds, beryllium gains two more bonds for a total of \(2 + 2 = 4\) bonds, and four bonding domains with no lone pairs on beryllium give a tetrahedral \(\text{AX}_4\) geometry of about \(109.5^\circ\). This is exactly the bridged tetrahedral environment found in solid \(\text{BeCl}_2\). (Choice A) This wrongly grants beryllium an expanded octet by adding three pairs to reach ten electrons; a period-2 element such as beryllium has no energetically accessible \(d\) orbitals and cannot exceed eight electrons, so it stops at the octet with four bonds rather than continuing to ten electrons and five bonds. (Choice B) This misidentifies the starting electron count by treating beryllium as if it were boron in \(\text{BF}_3\), beginning at a sextet of six electrons that needs only one more pair; beryllium has only two valence electrons and forms two bonds, so it begins at four electrons and requires two pairs, not one, and the resulting four-coordinate center is tetrahedral rather than trigonal planar. (Choice C) This correctly starts at four electrons but stops after accepting a single lone pair, reaching only \(4 + 2 = 6\) electrons; six electrons still fall short of an octet, so this fails the stated requirement that the solid attains a full octet. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the electron count of an electron-deficient center, the pair arithmetic needed to complete its octet, and the resulting coordination geometry to reach the answer.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '38d17142-e29b-4d2d-adc3-e1a44021dcc4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '38d17142-e29b-4d2d-adc3-e1a44021dcc4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('38d17142-e29b-4d2d-adc3-e1a44021dcc4', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Applies expanded-octet (period-3 d-orbital) reasoning to a period-2 atom, overshooting to 10 electrons and five bonds$a$),
  ('38d17142-e29b-4d2d-adc3-e1a44021dcc4', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Treats Be as the boron/BF3 sextet case (starts at 6 electrons, needs only one pair), yielding trigonal-planar 3-coordinate instead of recognizing Be's 4-electron quartet start$a$),
  ('38d17142-e29b-4d2d-adc3-e1a44021dcc4', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Correctly starts at 4 electrons but stops after accepting only one lone pair (reaching 6), halting the multi-step path one pair short of the octet$a$);

-- 16. [Octet Rule & Exceptions · Skill 2 · ans C] id d3ed9301-3e41-47a7-9018-9a8b2f467f0a
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist drawing \(\text{XeF}_4\) starts from the 36 valence electrons, places four \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) single bonds, and completes each fluorine octet with three lone pairs. A colleague objects that the central xenon now violates the octet rule and proposes converting a fluorine lone pair into a \(\text{Xe}=\text{F}\) double bond to "fix" it. Evaluating both structures by formal charge, which assessment of the original all-single-bond structure is most defensible?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"It is preferred, but only because xenon ends with 10 electrons rather than 12; the leftover after the bonds and fluorine octets is a single lone pair, giving xenon a formal charge of \\(+2\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"It is inferior: the double-bond structure restores xenon to a true octet of 8 electrons and gives every atom a formal charge of \\(0\\), so the all-single-bond structure should be discarded."},{"label":"C","text":"It is preferred: xenon carries an expanded octet of 12 electrons yet every atom holds a formal charge of \\(0\\), whereas the double-bond structure introduces \\(-1\\) on xenon and \\(+1\\) on the double-bonded fluorine, a worse formal-charge distribution that buys no octet."},{"label":"D","text":"Both structures are equivalent: xenon retains a complete octet of 8 electrons in each, so converting a lone pair to a double bond changes neither the electron count nor any formal charge."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the all-single-bond structure keeps every formal charge at zero while the double-bond "fix" separates charge without ever restoring an octet. Step 1 (electron bookkeeping): \(\text{Xe}\) contributes 8 and four \(\text{F}\) contribute \(4 \times 7 = 28\), for 36 total electrons (18 pairs); the four \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) single bonds use 4 pairs and the four fluorine octets use \(4 \times 3 = 12\) pairs, accounting for 16 pairs (32 electrons), so the remaining 2 pairs (4 electrons) localize on \(\text{Xe}\). Step 2 (expanded octet): \(\text{Xe}\) then carries 4 bonding pairs plus 2 lone pairs, i.e. 12 electrons, an expanded octet that period-5 \(\text{Xe}\) can accommodate. Step 3 (formal charge of the single-bond form): \(\text{FC}_{\text{Xe}} = 8 - 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(8) = 0\) and each \(\text{F}\) gives \(7 - 6 - \tfrac{1}{2}(2) = 0\), so every formal charge is zero. Step 4 (the double-bond "fix"): to make one \(\text{Xe}=\text{F}\) double bond while conserving all 36 electrons, that fluorine must donate one of its lone pairs into the new bond, leaving \(\text{Xe}\) with 3 single bonds, 1 double bond, and 2 lone pairs, i.e. \(10 + 4 = 14\) electrons — not an octet but an even larger expanded shell. Now \(\text{FC}_{\text{Xe}} = 8 - 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(10) = -1\) and the double-bonded \(\text{F}\) becomes \(7 - 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(4) = +1\) (the three single-bonded \(\text{F}\) remain \(0\)), so the charges sum to \(0\) as required for a neutral molecule but are no longer all zero. Because the single-bond structure achieves all-zero formal charges and the double-bond structure forces a \(-1/+1\) separation while still failing to give xenon an octet, the original structure is preferred. (Choice A) This undercounts the leftover by one pair: \(36 - 32 = 4\) electrons remain (two lone pairs, 12 electrons on \(\text{Xe}\)), not one lone pair, and the correct formal charge on \(\text{Xe}\) in the single-bond form is \(0\), not \(+2\). (Choice B) The proposed double bond does not restore an octet on xenon, which actually rises to 14 electrons, and it creates a \(-1/+1\) formal-charge separation rather than the all-zero set claimed, so it cannot make the all-single-bond structure inferior. (Choice D) Xenon does not retain a plain octet in either structure; the 4 electrons that have nowhere else to go force an expanded octet of 12 in the single-bond form and of 14 once a double bond is made, and that conversion changes the formal charges from all-zero to a \(-1/+1\) pair. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a total-electron count, an expanded-octet determination, and a charge-conserving formal-charge comparison to judge which Lewis structure is governed by the lowest formal charges.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'd3ed9301-3e41-47a7-9018-9a8b2f467f0a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd3ed9301-3e41-47a7-9018-9a8b2f467f0a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d3ed9301-3e41-47a7-9018-9a8b2f467f0a', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Leftover-electron undercount: subtracts correctly to 32 used but treats the 4 remaining electrons as a single lone pair (10 electrons on Xe), then propagates that miscount into a wrong +2 formal charge on the central atom.$a$),
  ('d3ed9301-3e41-47a7-9018-9a8b2f467f0a', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Octet-rule purist: assumes the central atom must reach exactly 8 electrons and that a double bond restores a true octet with all-zero formal charges, missing that the charge-conserving double bond drives Xe to 14 electrons and creates a -1/+1 separation.$a$),
  ('d3ed9301-3e41-47a7-9018-9a8b2f467f0a', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Octet-conservation error: assumes the central atom holds a fixed octet of 8 in any valid structure, so it concludes the bond/lone-pair swap is electronically and formally inconsequential.$a$);

-- 17. [Resonance & Delocalization · Skill 2 · ans C] id 4463d034-3f0f-42fa-b161-386a205714f2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Four carbon–oxygen linkages are compared: the \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) bond in dimethyl ether \(\left(\text{CH}_3-\text{O}-\text{CH}_3\right)\), the carbon–oxygen bonds in the carbonate ion \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\) (one \(\text{C=O}\) and two \(\text{C}-\text{O}^-\), three equivalent resonance contributors), the carbon–oxygen bonds in the formate ion \(\text{HCOO}^-\) (one \(\text{C=O}\) and one \(\text{C}-\text{O}^-\), two equivalent contributors), and the \(\text{C=O}\) bond in formaldehyde \(\left(\text{H}_2\text{C=O}\right)\). Because every linkage joins the same pair of atoms, length tracks the resonance-averaged bond order. Ranked from longest to shortest, these four \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) distances most likely fall in which order?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{CO}_3^{2-} > \\text{HCOO}^- > \\text{dimethyl ether} > \\text{formaldehyde}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{dimethyl ether} > \\text{HCOO}^- > \\text{CO}_3^{2-} > \\text{formaldehyde}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{dimethyl ether} > \\text{CO}_3^{2-} > \\text{HCOO}^- > \\text{formaldehyde}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{formaldehyde} > \\text{HCOO}^- > \\text{CO}_3^{2-} > \\text{dimethyl ether}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the four linkages must first be converted to resonance-averaged \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) bond orders and then ordered by length, with the recognition that, for a fixed atom pair, a higher bond order means a shorter, stronger bond. The dimethyl ether \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) is a localized single bond of order \(1.0\). In carbonate, one \(\pi\) pair is delocalized over three equivalent positions, so each \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) bond order is \(\left(1\ \text{double} + 2\ \text{single}\right)/3 = 4/3 \approx 1.33\). In formate, one \(\pi\) pair is delocalized over only two positions, so each \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) bond order is \(\left(1\ \text{double} + 1\ \text{single}\right)/2 = 3/2 = 1.50\). The formaldehyde \(\text{C=O}\) is a localized double bond of order \(2.0\). The ascending bond-order sequence is therefore \(1.0 < 1.33 < 1.50 < 2.0\), so the descending length sequence (longest to shortest) is dimethyl ether \(>\) carbonate \(>\) formate \(>\) formaldehyde, which is choice B. (Choice A) This ordering follows from the misconception that delocalization erases all double-bond character, turning the carbonate and formate bonds into pure single bonds, and then ranking those "single" bonds by their number of resonance contributors so that carbonate (three contributors) is placed longest; in fact delocalization distributes double-bond character rather than removing it, giving bond orders of \(1.33\) and \(1.50\) that are both greater than the localized single bond of dimethyl ether, which must be the longest. (Choice B) This ordering inverts the two ions by assuming that spreading the \(\pi\) pair over more oxygen positions raises the per-bond order, making carbonate shorter than formate; the opposite holds, because dividing one \(\pi\) pair among three positions \(\left(4/3\right)\) yields a smaller per-bond order than dividing it among two \(\left(3/2\right)\), so carbonate is the longer of the two. (Choice D) This ordering reverses the relationship between bond order and bond length by treating a higher bond order as producing a longer bond; for a fixed atom pair the relationship is inverse, so the order-\(2.0\) formaldehyde \(\text{C=O}\) is the shortest, not the longest, distance. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must compute two fractional resonance bond orders, anchor them between a localized single and a localized double bond, and convert the resulting bond-order ranking into a length ranking using the inverse bond-order–bond-length relationship.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '4463d034-3f0f-42fa-b161-386a205714f2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4463d034-3f0f-42fa-b161-386a205714f2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4463d034-3f0f-42fa-b161-386a205714f2', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Believes delocalization removes all double-bond character (bonds become pure singles), then ranks the 'single' bonds by their count of resonance contributors, placing the three-contributor carbonate as longest.$a$),
  ('4463d034-3f0f-42fa-b161-386a205714f2', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Inverts the two ions by assuming the pi pair spread over more oxygen positions (carbonate, 3) gives a higher per-bond order than the one spread over fewer (formate, 2), miscomputing 4/3 as larger than 3/2.$a$),
  ('4463d034-3f0f-42fa-b161-386a205714f2', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the inverse bond-order to bond-length rule, treating the highest bond order (the localized C=O double bond) as the longest distance instead of the shortest.$a$);

-- 18. [Resonance & Delocalization · Skill 2 · ans D] id 6291d2de-0a73-474c-a247-72281cc92924
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$The azide ion \(\text{N}_3^-\) is linear with the central nitrogen flanked by two terminal nitrogens, and it is best described as a resonance hybrid of several octet-obeying contributors. One contributor is symmetric, \(\text{N}{=}\text{N}{=}\text{N}\), while two others are asymmetric mirror images of the form \(\text{N}{\equiv}\text{N}{-}\text{N}\). Choosing the dominant contributor by formal charge, which statement most accurately describes the bonding in the actual ion?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"The two asymmetric forms together dominate while the symmetric form is negligible, so the two terminal nitrogen-nitrogen bonds are most likely unequal, with bond orders near 3 and near 1."},{"label":"B","text":"A single asymmetric form \\(\\text{N}{\\equiv}\\text{N}{-}\\text{N}\\) (charges \\(0, +1, -2\\)) dominates, so one terminal nitrogen-nitrogen bond is most likely much shorter than the other."},{"label":"C","text":"The symmetric form \\(\\text{N}{=}\\text{N}{=}\\text{N}\\) dominates, so the two terminal nitrogen-nitrogen bonds are most likely equal in length, each with bond order near 3."},{"label":"D","text":"The symmetric form \\(\\text{N}{=}\\text{N}{=}\\text{N}\\) (charges \\(-1, +1, -1\\)) dominates, so the two terminal nitrogen-nitrogen bonds are most likely equal in length, each with bond order near 2."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the dominant contributor must be selected by formal charge and then translated into a bond-length and bond-order prediction. Using \(\text{FC} = V - (\text{nonbonding } e^-) - \tfrac{1}{2}(\text{bonding } e^-)\) on the symmetric form \(\text{N}{=}\text{N}{=}\text{N}\): each terminal nitrogen has two lone pairs and one double bond \((5 - 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(4) = -1)\), and the central nitrogen has no lone pairs and two double bonds \((5 - 0 - \tfrac{1}{2}(8) = +1)\), giving charges \(-1, +1, -1\) with no atom exceeding magnitude 1. For an asymmetric form \(\text{N}{\equiv}\text{N}{-}\text{N}\): the triple-bonded terminal nitrogen has one lone pair \((5 - 2 - \tfrac{1}{2}(6) = 0)\), the central nitrogen has no lone pairs \((5 - 0 - \tfrac{1}{2}(8) = +1)\), and the single-bonded terminal nitrogen has three lone pairs \((5 - 6 - \tfrac{1}{2}(2) = -2)\), giving charges \(0, +1, -2\). The smallest-magnitude rule favors the contributor whose formal charges are closest to zero, so the symmetric \(\text{N}{=}\text{N}{=}\text{N}\) (largest magnitude 1) outweighs each asymmetric form (largest magnitude 2). Because that dominant contributor is symmetric about the central nitrogen, the two terminal nitrogen-nitrogen bonds are equivalent; even accounting for the two minor asymmetric contributors, those two are exact mirror images and therefore contribute equally, preserving the equivalence of the two bonds. Each terminal bond is a double bond in the dominant form, so its net bond order in the hybrid sits near 2, and the two bonds are equal in length. This matches the measured azide ion, which has two equal nitrogen-nitrogen bond lengths. (Choice A) This treats the symmetric contributor as negligible and the asymmetric ones as dominant, reversing the formal-charge preference; it also wrongly predicts unequal bonds, but because the two asymmetric forms are equivalent mirror images they contribute equally and average to two identical terminal bonds rather than one short and one long. (Choice B) This selects a single asymmetric contributor as dominant, but its \(-2\) formal charge exceeds the magnitude-1 charges of the symmetric form, so the smallest-magnitude rule excludes it as the major contributor; moreover, selecting one asymmetric form alone improperly ignores its degenerate mirror image, which would have to contribute equally. (Choice C) This correctly identifies the symmetric form and the equality of the two bonds but assigns bond order near 3; in \(\text{N}{=}\text{N}{=}\text{N}\) each terminal bond is a double bond, so the net terminal bond order is near 2, not 3. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a formal-charge calculation across multiple structures, apply the smallest-magnitude rule to rank contributors, recognize how molecular symmetry forces degenerate contributors to be weighted equally, and convert bond order into a bond-length comparison to reach the answer.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '6291d2de-0a73-474c-a247-72281cc92924';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6291d2de-0a73-474c-a247-72281cc92924';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6291d2de-0a73-474c-a247-72281cc92924', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the formal-charge preference by treating the asymmetric forms as dominant over the symmetric one and concludes the terminal bonds are unequal$a$),
  ('6291d2de-0a73-474c-a247-72281cc92924', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Picks the asymmetric contributor with a -2 charge as dominant, ignoring the smallest-formal-charge rule and the required degenerate mirror image, predicting unequal terminal bonds$a$),
  ('6291d2de-0a73-474c-a247-72281cc92924', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Correctly identifies the symmetric dominant form and equal bonds but misreads the double bonds as giving terminal bond order near 3 instead of near 2$a$);

-- 19. [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · Skill 2 · ans A] id 75cb46b6-c532-4874-9c01-c69c61633e6b
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$In acetamide, \(\text{CH}_3\text{-C(=O)-NH}_2\), the nitrogen is bonded to two hydrogens and to the carbonyl carbon and carries one lone pair. Spectroscopy shows that rotation about the \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) bond is strongly hindered and that the three atoms bonded to nitrogen lie in essentially the same plane, with their bond angles summing to nearly \(360^\circ\). Which assignment for the nitrogen most likely accounts for these two observations together?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(sp^2\\) hybridized, with the lone pair occupying the unhybridized \\(p\\) orbital so it can overlap with the carbonyl \\(\\pi\\) system"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(sp^3\\) hybridized, with the lone pair occupying one \\(sp^3\\) hybrid orbital, giving a pyramidal nitrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(sp^2\\) hybridized, with the lone pair occupying one of the three \\(sp^2\\) hybrid orbitals in the nitrogen plane"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(sp\\) hybridized, with the lone pair occupying an \\(sp\\) hybrid orbital and the two \\(\\text{N}-\\text{H}\\) bonds using the unhybridized \\(p\\) orbitals"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the two observations can only be reconciled when the nitrogen lone pair is delocalized into the carbonyl, which forces nitrogen to be \(sp^2\) with the lone pair held in its unhybridized \(p\) orbital. Step one is the naive electron-domain count: nitrogen forms three sigma bonds (two \(\text{N}-\text{H}\) and one \(\text{N}-\text{C}\)) and holds one lone pair, which is four domains and would predict \(sp^3\). Step two is recognizing the controlling constraint supplied by the stem: hindered \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) rotation and a planar nitrogen whose angles sum to \(\approx 360^\circ\) are the signatures of amide resonance, \(\text{N}-\text{C}{=}\text{O} \leftrightarrow \text{N}^{+}{=}\text{C}-\text{O}^{-}\), in which the lone pair is donated into the \(\text{C}=\text{O}\) \(\pi\) system. Step three chains these together: for that lone pair to overlap with the carbonyl \(\pi\) system, it must sit in a \(p\) orbital aligned parallel to the \(\text{C}=\text{O}\) \(\pi\) bond, leaving nitrogen with three \(sp^2\) hybrids for its three sigma bonds. This gives trigonal-planar nitrogen (angles summing to \(\approx 360^\circ\)) and partial double-bond character in \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) (restricted rotation), matching both observations. (Choice B) is the result of stopping at the raw domain count of four and ignoring the resonance evidence in the stem; an \(sp^3\) nitrogen with a lone pair in a hybrid orbital would be pyramidal and would not place its lone pair in a \(p\) orbital able to conjugate, so the \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) bond would rotate freely, contradicting the data. (Choice C) reaches the correct \(sp^2\) hybridization but misplaces the lone pair into an in-plane \(sp^2\) hybrid; a lone pair confined to an \(sp^2\) hybrid lies in the nitrogen plane and is orthogonal to the carbonyl \(\pi\) system, so it could not delocalize and would not produce restricted rotation. (Choice D) over-reduces the domain count to two, an assignment reserved for a center with two sigma domains and no available lone-pair domain such as a terminal nitrile nitrogen; \(sp\) hybridization cannot accommodate three sigma bonds plus a conjugating lone pair and predicts a linear arrangement inconsistent with the observed planar three-coordinate geometry. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the electron-domain rule for hybridization with amide resonance and the geometric evidence in the stem to identify which orbital actually holds the nitrogen lone pair.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '75cb46b6-c532-4874-9c01-c69c61633e6b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '75cb46b6-c532-4874-9c01-c69c61633e6b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('75cb46b6-c532-4874-9c01-c69c61633e6b', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Applies the raw sigma-plus-lone-pair domain count (four domains to sp3) while ignoring the resonance/planarity evidence that forces the amide nitrogen to be sp2 and planar$a$),
  ('75cb46b6-c532-4874-9c01-c69c61633e6b', 'C', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly derives sp2 from the planar geometry but places the lone pair in an in-plane sp2 hybrid rather than the unhybridized p orbital, so it cannot conjugate with the carbonyl pi system$a$),
  ('75cb46b6-c532-4874-9c01-c69c61633e6b', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Over-reduces the hybridization to sp (two domains) by misapplying the rule that pi conjugation lowers the hybrid-domain count, an assignment that fits a terminal nitrile nitrogen, not a three-coordinate amide nitrogen$a$);

-- 20. [Sigma/Pi Bonds & Hybridization · Skill 2 · ans D] id d38b1ca7-2332-4aa4-b154-1a729d9ac0f2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A neutral, acyclic compound has the molecular formula \( \text{C}_3\text{H}_3\text{N} \) and is known to contain exactly one carbon-carbon double bond and one nitrile \( \left( \text{C} \equiv \text{N} \right) \) group, with no other multiple bonds. For the single structure consistent with these constraints, which pairing most likely gives the total number of \( \pi \) (pi) bonds in the molecule together with the number of atoms (counting both carbon and nitrogen) that are \( sp \) hybridized?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\( 3\\ \\pi \\) bonds and \\( 3\\ sp \\)-hybridized atoms"},{"label":"B","text":"\\( 2\\ \\pi \\) bonds and \\( 2\\ sp \\)-hybridized atoms"},{"label":"C","text":"\\( 4\\ \\pi \\) bonds and \\( 1\\ sp \\)-hybridized atom"},{"label":"D","text":"\\( 3\\ \\pi \\) bonds and \\( 2\\ sp \\)-hybridized atoms"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the structure must first be fixed from the formula and constraints, and only then can the \( \pi \) bonds and \( sp \)-hybridized atoms be tallied. The degree of unsaturation of \( \text{C}_3\text{H}_3\text{N} \) is \( \tfrac{1}{2}\left(2(3) + 2 + 1 - 3\right) = 3 \): a carbon-carbon double bond accounts for one degree, and the only way an acyclic molecule with no other multiple bonds can supply the remaining two degrees is a carbon-nitrogen triple bond, so the compound is acrylonitrile, \( \text{H}_2\text{C} = \text{CH} - \text{C} \equiv \text{N} \). The \( \text{C} = \text{C} \) double bond contributes one \( \pi \) bond and the \( \text{C} \equiv \text{N} \) triple bond contributes two \( \pi \) bonds, giving \( 1 + 2 = 3\ \pi \) bonds total. Hybridization follows from steric number: the terminal \( =\text{CH}_2 \) carbon and the internal \( =\text{CH}- \) carbon each have three electron domains and are \( sp^2 \), whereas the nitrile carbon has two domains (one \( \sigma \) to the internal carbon, one \( \sigma \) to nitrogen) and the terminal nitrogen has two domains (one \( \sigma \) bond plus one lone pair); both of those are \( sp \) hybridized, for \( 2\ sp \)-hybridized atoms. (Choice A) This counts the \( \pi \) bonds correctly but assigns three \( sp \)-hybridized atoms by treating the internal \( =\text{CH}- \) carbon as \( sp \); that carbon has three electron domains and is \( sp^2 \), so only the nitrile carbon and the terminal nitrogen are \( sp \). (Choice B) This drops the \( \pi \) bond of the \( \text{C} = \text{C} \) double bond and counts only the two \( \pi \) bonds of the triple bond; both the double and the triple bond contribute \( \pi \) bonds, so the correct total is three, not two. (Choice C) This assigns all three lines of the \( \text{C} \equiv \text{N} \) triple bond as \( \pi \) bonds and adds the \( \text{C} = \text{C} \) \( \pi \) bond to reach four, and it counts only the nitrile carbon as \( sp \); a triple bond is one \( \sigma \) plus two \( \pi \) because the first bond between two atoms is always a \( \sigma \) bond, and the terminal nitrogen with one \( \sigma \) bond and one lone pair is also \( sp \), so two atoms are \( sp \). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the degree-of-unsaturation deduction, the bond-multiplicity \( \pi \)-bond count, and the steric-number hybridization assignment to reach a single consistent answer.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'd38b1ca7-2332-4aa4-b154-1a729d9ac0f2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd38b1ca7-2332-4aa4-b154-1a729d9ac0f2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d38b1ca7-2332-4aa4-b154-1a729d9ac0f2', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Correct pi count but misassigns the internal sp2 vinyl carbon as sp, inflating the sp-atom count to three$a$),
  ('d38b1ca7-2332-4aa4-b154-1a729d9ac0f2', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Counts only the triple bond's two pi bonds and forgets the C=C double bond contributes a pi bond$a$),
  ('d38b1ca7-2332-4aa4-b154-1a729d9ac0f2', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Treats every line of the triple bond as a pi bond (3 pi) plus the C=C pi (4 total) and counts only the nitrile carbon, omitting the sp nitrogen$a$);

-- 21. [Stereochemistry · Skill 2 · ans B] id 3056c12f-e773-404b-a285-a3be9652be0b
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist prepares every possible stereoisomer of 2,3,4,5-tetrabromohexane, \(\text{CH}_3\text{CHBrCHBrCHBrCHBrCH}_3\), in which C2, C3, C4, and C5 are each bonded to four different groups. The molecule has a mirror plane that bisects the C3–C4 bond, relating the C2–C3 half to the C5–C4 half. Counting only distinct compounds, how many separable stereoisomers exist in total, and how many of those will most likely show no optical rotation when measured as a pure substance?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(16\\) stereoisomers in total, of which \\(0\\) are optically inactive."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(10\\) stereoisomers in total, of which \\(2\\) are optically inactive."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(8\\) stereoisomers in total, of which \\(2\\) are optically inactive."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(16\\) stereoisomers in total, of which \\(2\\) are optically inactive."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the count must be corrected for the molecule's internal symmetry rather than read off the naive \(2^n\) rule. Step 1: with four stereocenters, the raw number of (R/S) label combinations is \(2^4 = 16\). Step 2: because the molecule is symmetric (the mirror plane bisects the C3–C4 bond, so numbering the chain from either end is equivalent), every label set and its end-for-end relabeling describe the same compound; this collapses the 16 raw labels into fewer distinct molecules. Of the 16 labels, four are palindromic and map onto themselves (e.g. the (2R,3R,4R,5R), (2S,3S,4S,5S), (2R,3S,4S,5R), and (2S,3R,4R,5S) sets), while the remaining twelve pair up into six, giving \(4 + 6 = 10\) distinct stereoisomers. Step 3: a stereoisomer is optically inactive when it is superimposable on its mirror image (meso) because the internal mirror plane makes one half the mirror image of the other; testing each of the 10, exactly two satisfy this—(2R,3R,4S,5S) and (2R,3S,4R,5S)—so their internal reflection cancels any rotation. The other eight are chiral and form four enantiomeric pairs that are each optically active. Thus there are 10 stereoisomers total, 2 of which (the meso forms) are optically inactive. (Choice A) Taking \(2^4 = 16\) as the total and reporting zero inactive forms ignores the internal mirror plane entirely; this both overcounts distinct compounds (it never collapses the end-for-end duplicate labelings) and misses that symmetric (R/...) versus (S/...) halves generate meso forms, so it is doubly in error. (Choice C) Reporting 8 total comes from correctly spotting the 2 meso forms but then dividing the full set of 16 labels in half as if the mirror plane removed exactly one partner for every arrangement; that shortcut is the rule for a molecule whose labels are all non-self-symmetric, and it wrongly halves even the four palindromic (self-paired) labels, undercounting the true total of 10. (Choice D) Reporting 16 total with 2 inactive correctly identifies the two meso compounds by the symmetry test but fails to merge the end-for-end duplicate labelings into single compounds, leaving the inflated raw count of 16 instead of the 10 separable substances. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the stereocenter count, the symmetry-driven collapse of duplicate configurations, and the meso/achiral criterion to arrive at both numbers.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '3056c12f-e773-404b-a285-a3be9652be0b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3056c12f-e773-404b-a285-a3be9652be0b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3056c12f-e773-404b-a285-a3be9652be0b', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Applies the naive 2^n rule and ignores internal symmetry, so no meso forms are recognized and no duplicate labelings are collapsed (16 total, 0 inactive).$a$),
  ('3056c12f-e773-404b-a285-a3be9652be0b', 'C', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly finds the 2 meso forms but mis-corrects the chiral count by halving all 16 labels, including the self-symmetric ones (8 total, 2 inactive).$a$),
  ('3056c12f-e773-404b-a285-a3be9652be0b', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Identifies the 2 meso forms via the symmetry test but skips collapsing the end-for-end duplicate labelings, leaving the inflated raw count (16 total, 2 inactive).$a$);

-- 22. [Stereochemistry · Skill 2 · ans C] id f0d31ad3-35ab-4043-9e85-e01853b6be12
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A tetrasubstituted alkene has the connectivity \((\text{OHC})(\text{HOCH}_2)\text{C}=\text{C}(\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH})(\text{C}(\text{CH}_3){=}\text{CH}_2)\): the left alkene carbon bears a formyl group \(-\text{CHO}\) and a hydroxymethyl group \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\), and the right alkene carbon bears a 2-hydroxyethyl group \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) and an isopropenyl group \(-\text{C}(\text{CH}_3){=}\text{CH}_2\). The \(-\text{CHO}\) group and the \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) group lie on the same side of the \(\text{C}=\text{C}\) axis. Applying CIP priority to each alkene carbon, the configuration is most likely$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(Z\\), because \\(-\\text{CHO}\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}\\) on the left carbon, while on the right carbon \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}\\) outranks \\(-\\text{C}(\\text{CH}_3){=}\\text{CH}_2\\) because its chain carries an oxygen atom, placing the two higher-priority groups on the same side."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(Z\\), because \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CHO}\\) on the left carbon since it carries a full \\(-\\text{OH}\\) substituent, while \\(-\\text{C}(\\text{CH}_3){=}\\text{CH}_2\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}\\) on the right carbon, placing the two higher-priority groups on the same side."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(E\\), because \\(-\\text{CHO}\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}\\) on the left carbon and \\(-\\text{C}(\\text{CH}_3){=}\\text{CH}_2\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}\\) on the right carbon, placing the two higher-priority groups on opposite sides."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(Z\\), because \\(-\\text{CHO}\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}\\) on the left carbon and \\(-\\text{C}(\\text{CH}_3){=}\\text{CH}_2\\) outranks \\(-\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}\\) on the right carbon, and these two higher-priority groups therefore lie on the same side."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because CIP priority must be settled separately on each alkene carbon using the duplicated-atom rule, and only then can the stated geometry be translated into a descriptor. On the left carbon both substituents begin with carbon, so one compares the atom sets on that first carbon: the formyl carbon of \(-\text{CHO}\) is double-bonded to oxygen, so its set is \((\text{O}, \text{O}, \text{H})\) once the \(\text{C}{=}\text{O}\) oxygen is duplicated, whereas the carbon of \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) gives \((\text{O}, \text{H}, \text{H})\); at the first point of difference \((\text{O}\) versus \(\text{H})\), \(-\text{CHO}\) wins, so \(-\text{CHO} > -\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\). On the right carbon both substituents also begin with carbon: the isopropenyl carbon of \(-\text{C}(\text{CH}_3){=}\text{CH}_2\) is double-bonded to \(\text{CH}_2\) and single-bonded to \(\text{CH}_3\), so with the \(\text{C}{=}\text{C}\) carbon duplicated its set is \((\text{C}, \text{C}, \text{C})\), whereas the first carbon of \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) gives \((\text{C}, \text{H}, \text{H})\); at the first point of difference \((\text{C}\) versus \(\text{H})\), the isopropenyl group wins, so \(-\text{C}(\text{CH}_3){=}\text{CH}_2 > -\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\). The stem fixes \(-\text{CHO}\) (the higher-priority group on the left) on the same side as \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) (the lower-priority group on the right). Therefore \(-\text{CHO}\) is on the opposite side from \(-\text{C}(\text{CH}_3){=}\text{CH}_2\), the higher-priority group on the right, so the two higher-priority groups are trans, which defines the \(E\) configuration. (Choice A) This treats the oxygen buried two atoms deep in \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) as decisive, but CIP priority is set by the first point of difference: the right-carbon comparison \((\text{C}, \text{C}, \text{C})\) for the isopropenyl group versus \((\text{C}, \text{H}, \text{H})\) for the 2-hydroxyethyl group is resolved at the first attached carbon, where the isopropenyl group wins; the distant oxygen never enters the comparison, so \(-\text{C}(\text{CH}_3){=}\text{CH}_2\) is the higher priority and the descriptor is \(E\), not \(Z\). (Choice B) This omits the duplicated-atom rule for the \(\text{C}{=}\text{O}\) of \(-\text{CHO}\); the formyl carbon presents \((\text{O}, \text{O}, \text{H})\) once the carbonyl oxygen is counted twice, which outranks the \((\text{O}, \text{H}, \text{H})\) of \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\), so \(-\text{CHO}\) is the higher-priority group on the left and the geometry resolves to \(E\). (Choice D) The two priority assignments are correct, but the conclusion misreads the geometry: the stem places the higher-priority \(-\text{CHO}\) on the same side as the lower-priority \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\), which puts the two higher-priority groups on opposite sides, the \(E\) arrangement, not the same side. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the duplicated-atom rule applied to two different multiple bonds with the first-point-of-difference rule and then map a mixed higher/lower-priority geometric constraint onto the correct \(E\)/\(Z\) descriptor.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'f0d31ad3-35ab-4043-9e85-e01853b6be12';
