INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f0d31ad3-35ab-4043-9e85-e01853b6be12', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Chasing a distant oxygen on the right carbon instead of applying the first-point-of-difference rule, wrongly ranking -CH2CH2OH above the isopropenyl group and flipping the descriptor to Z.$a$),
  ('f0d31ad3-35ab-4043-9e85-e01853b6be12', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Failing to apply the duplicated-atom rule to the C=O of the formyl group, wrongly ranking -CH2OH above -CHO on the left carbon and concluding Z.$a$),
  ('f0d31ad3-35ab-4043-9e85-e01853b6be12', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Assigning both CIP priorities correctly but misreading the stated geometry, claiming the two higher-priority groups lie on the same side when the mixed high/low constraint places them on opposite sides.$a$);

-- 23. [VSEPR & Molecular Geometry · Skill 2 · ans C] id 6a39100d-03ce-42b6-8624-70d18cb2c8bd
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist surveys four gas-phase species, each built from a single central atom bonded to exactly two identical terminal atoms by single bonds: \(\text{BeCl}_2\), \(\text{OF}_2\), \(\text{SnCl}_2\), and \(\text{XeF}_2\). Only one of the four has a central atom that both carries at least one lone pair and still leaves the molecule linear. Which species most likely fits that combined description?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{BeCl}_2\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{OF}_2\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{XeF}_2\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{SnCl}_2\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because only \(\text{XeF}_2\) combines a lone-pair-bearing central atom with a linear molecular shape. For each species the steric number is the count of bonding regions plus lone pairs on the central atom, found by tallying the central atom's valence electrons and subtracting the one electron it contributes to each single bond. Beryllium supplies \(2\) valence electrons, both used in the two \(\text{Be}-\text{Cl}\) bonds, so \(0\) lone pairs remain and the steric number is \(2\) (\(\text{AX}_2\)): \(\text{BeCl}_2\) is linear but its central atom holds no lone pair. Oxygen supplies \(6\) electrons; \(2\) go into the two \(\text{O}-\text{F}\) bonds, leaving \(4\) electrons as \(2\) lone pairs for a steric number of \(4\) (\(\text{AX}_2\text{E}_2\)), giving a bent molecule near \(103^\circ\). Tin (group 14, behaving as a \(+2\) center) leaves \(2\) of its \(4\) valence electrons as one lone pair after forming two \(\text{Sn}-\text{Cl}\) bonds, a steric number of \(3\) (\(\text{AX}_2\text{E}_1\)), which is also bent. Xenon supplies \(8\) electrons; \(2\) form the two \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) bonds and the remaining \(6\) become \(3\) lone pairs, a steric number of \(5\) (\(\text{AX}_2\text{E}_3\)). VSEPR seats the three lone pairs in the equatorial sites of the trigonal bipyramid, forcing the two fluorines into the axial positions \(180^\circ\) apart, so \(\text{XeF}_2\) is linear even though its central atom carries three lone pairs; this is the only species satisfying both conditions. (Choice A) \(\text{BeCl}_2\) is genuinely linear, but with steric number \(2\) and no lone pairs on beryllium it fails the requirement that the central atom carry at least one lone pair. (Choice B) \(\text{OF}_2\) has two lone pairs on oxygen, satisfying the lone-pair clause, but its steric number of \(4\) makes it \(\text{AX}_2\text{E}_2\) and therefore bent, not linear. (Choice D) \(\text{SnCl}_2\) carries one lone pair on tin, again meeting the lone-pair clause, but its steric number of \(3\) makes it \(\text{AX}_2\text{E}_1\) and bent rather than linear. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must compute a steric number for each candidate, translate it into the electron-domain and molecular geometry, and test all four against a two-part controlling constraint to isolate the single species that is both lone-pair-bearing and linear.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '6a39100d-03ce-42b6-8624-70d18cb2c8bd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6a39100d-03ce-42b6-8624-70d18cb2c8bd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6a39100d-03ce-42b6-8624-70d18cb2c8bd', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Identifies a linear molecule but drops the lone-pair clause, choosing the trivially linear AX2 case (BeCl2) and ignoring that beryllium has no lone pair$a$),
  ('6a39100d-03ce-42b6-8624-70d18cb2c8bd', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Assumes two bonds automatically means linear and stops before placing lone pairs, mislabeling the bent AX2E2 species (OF2) as linear$a$),
  ('6a39100d-03ce-42b6-8624-70d18cb2c8bd', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly finds a lone pair on the central atom but miscomputes or skips the steric number, treating the bent AX2E1 species (SnCl2) as if it were linear$a$);

-- 24. [VSEPR & Molecular Geometry · Skill 2 · ans B] id 90e5b487-f372-4672-b7e0-959b44688d56
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A spectroscopist tabulates one experimentally measured bond angle for each of four fluorides whose central atom bears at least one lone pair: the \(\text{F}-\text{N}-\text{F}\) angle in \(\text{NF}_3\), the \(\text{F}-\text{O}-\text{F}\) angle in \(\text{OF}_2\), the equatorial \(\text{F}-\text{S}-\text{F}\) angle in \(\text{SF}_4\), and the axial \(\text{F}-\text{Cl}-\text{F}\) angle in \(\text{ClF}_3\). For each, she also notes the ideal angle predicted before lone-pair repulsion is taken into account. The measured value that is most likely to fall the farthest below its ideal VSEPR value is$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"the axial \\(\\text{F}-\\text{Cl}-\\text{F}\\) angle in \\(\\text{ClF}_3\\), where two equatorial lone pairs bend both axial bonds away from \\(180^\\circ\\) down to roughly \\(150^\\circ\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"the equatorial \\(\\text{F}-\\text{S}-\\text{F}\\) angle in \\(\\text{SF}_4\\), where a single equatorial lone pair presses on two equatorial bonds whose ideal separation is already only \\(120^\\circ\\), compressing them to roughly \\(102^\\circ\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"the \\(\\text{F}-\\text{O}-\\text{F}\\) angle in \\(\\text{OF}_2\\), where two lone pairs on oxygen compress the ideal \\(109.5^\\circ\\) angle to roughly \\(87^\\circ\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"the \\(\\text{F}-\\text{N}-\\text{F}\\) angle in \\(\\text{NF}_3\\), where a single lone pair on nitrogen compresses the ideal \\(109.5^\\circ\\) angle to roughly \\(88^\\circ\\)."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the size of a lone-pair-driven deviation depends on both how hard the lone pair pushes and how little room the affected bonds start with. In \(\text{SF}_4\) (steric number 5, see-saw), the one lone pair sits equatorial and presses on the two equatorial \(\text{S}-\text{F}\) bonds, whose ideal mutual angle is only \(120^\circ\); the bond pair–bond pair separation is already small, so the equatorial pair is squeezed down to about \(101\text{--}102^\circ\), a deviation of roughly \(18^\circ\). That is larger than any of the other listed deviations, which all start from wider ideal angles (\(180^\circ\) or \(109.5^\circ\)) and compress by only a handful of degrees. (Choice A) misstates the magnitude: the two equatorial lone pairs in T-shaped \(\text{ClF}_3\) bend the axial bonds inward only slightly, to about \(175^\circ\) (a deviation of roughly \(5^\circ\)), not to \(150^\circ\). (Choice C) overstates the effect in \(\text{OF}_2\); the two lone pairs on oxygen compress the angle only to about \(103^\circ\) (a deviation of roughly \(6\text{--}7^\circ\)), not to \(87^\circ\). (Choice D) likewise overstates \(\text{NF}_3\), where the single lone pair lowers the angle only to about \(102^\circ\) (a deviation of roughly \(7^\circ\)), not to \(88^\circ\). This question targets the AAMC skill of Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving, because it requires combining lone-pair repulsion magnitude with the starting ideal geometry to rank competing bond-angle distortions.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '90e5b487-f372-4672-b7e0-959b44688d56';
