INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('90e5b487-f372-4672-b7e0-959b44688d56', 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Correctly identifies the axial bonds are bent inward by equatorial lone pairs but grossly exaggerates the magnitude (claims ~150 deg instead of the real ~175 deg deviation of only ~5 deg)$a$),
  ('90e5b487-f372-4672-b7e0-959b44688d56', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Right qualitative cause (two lone pairs on O compress the angle) but invents an implausibly large compression to ~87 deg rather than the actual ~103 deg$a$),
  ('90e5b487-f372-4672-b7e0-959b44688d56', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Right qualitative cause (one lone pair on N compresses the tetrahedral angle) but fabricates a ~88 deg value far below the real ~102 deg, overstating the deviation$a$);

-- 25. [VSEPR & Molecular Geometry · Skill 4 · ans A] id 9d026d49-8c86-4be1-bade-4c1608ffeed2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist has four neutral fluorides whose central atoms each bear at least one lone pair: \(\text{SF}_4\), \(\text{ClF}_3\), \(\text{XeF}_4\), and \(\text{BrF}_5\). Every \(\text{X}-\text{F}\) bond in all four is polar. Working out the molecular geometry of each from its electron-domain count, which of these molecules will most likely have a net dipole moment of zero?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{XeF}_4\\), because its six electron domains place the two lone pairs on opposite axial sites and leave four \\(\\text{Xe}-\\text{F}\\) bonds in a square planar arrangement whose dipoles cancel"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{SF}_4\\), because its five electron domains give a symmetric seesaw shape in which the four \\(\\text{S}-\\text{F}\\) bond dipoles cancel"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{ClF}_3\\), because its five electron domains give a T-shaped arrangement in which the three \\(\\text{Cl}-\\text{F}\\) bond dipoles cancel"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{BrF}_5\\), because its six electron domains give a square pyramidal shape in which the five \\(\\text{Br}-\\text{F}\\) bond dipoles cancel"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because each molecule must first be resolved into its electron-domain count, then its molecular geometry, and only then judged for dipole cancellation. \(\text{XeF}_4\): xenon (8 valence electrons) forms four \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) bonds, leaving \((8-4)/2 = 2\) lone pairs, for 6 domains (an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_2\) case). In an octahedral electron arrangement the two lone pairs adopt opposite (trans) axial positions to minimize repulsion, which forces the four \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) bonds into a square plane at \(90^\circ\). The four equal bond dipoles point to the corners of a square and sum to zero, and the two opposed lone pairs are symmetric, so the molecule is nonpolar. The controlling idea is that the presence of lone pairs does not by itself make a molecule polar; only an asymmetric distribution of bonds and lone pairs leaves a net dipole, and \(\text{XeF}_4\) is the lone case here in which symmetry survives. (Choice B) In \(\text{SF}_4\) sulfur (6 valence electrons) forms four bonds and retains \((6-4)/2 = 1\) lone pair, giving 5 domains (\(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_1\)) and a seesaw shape; the equatorial lone pair makes the structure asymmetric, the two axial and two equatorial \(\text{S}-\text{F}\) dipoles do not cancel, and the molecule is polar. (Choice C) In \(\text{ClF}_3\) chlorine (7 valence electrons) forms three bonds and retains \((7-3)/2 = 2\) lone pairs, giving 5 domains (\(\text{AX}_3\text{E}_2\)) and a T-shape; the two equatorial lone pairs leave an asymmetric T in which the three \(\text{Cl}-\text{F}\) dipoles fail to cancel, so the molecule is polar. (Choice D) In \(\text{BrF}_5\) bromine (7 valence electrons) forms five bonds and retains \((7-5)/2 = 1\) lone pair, giving 6 domains (\(\text{AX}_5\text{E}_1\)) and a square pyramidal shape; the single apical lone pair leaves the apical and basal bond dipoles unbalanced, so a net dipole remains and the molecule is polar. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must carry an intermediate electron-domain count through to a geometry and then evaluate dipole cancellation for each of four molecules before selecting the one that is symmetric.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '9d026d49-8c86-4be1-bade-4c1608ffeed2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9d026d49-8c86-4be1-bade-4c1608ffeed2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9d026d49-8c86-4be1-bade-4c1608ffeed2', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Treats any high-symmetry-sounding shape (seesaw) as dipole-canceling while ignoring that the equatorial lone pair in the AX4E1 seesaw breaks symmetry and leaves SF4 polar$a$),
  ('9d026d49-8c86-4be1-bade-4c1608ffeed2', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Assumes the T-shaped AX3E2 arrangement is symmetric enough to cancel its three bond dipoles, overlooking that two equatorial lone pairs make ClF3 asymmetric and polar$a$),
  ('9d026d49-8c86-4be1-bade-4c1608ffeed2', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly derives 6 domains and square pyramidal geometry for BrF5 but wrongly concludes the five bond dipoles cancel, ignoring that the apical lone pair leaves an unbalanced axial dipole$a$);

